AB  - The purpose of this study was to examine the self-reported effects of professional training on the effectiveness of substance abuse counselors in providing services to clients with dual disorders in rural communities. Dual diagnosis or mentally ill substance abuser (MISA) is a term used to describe clients with co-occurring substance abuse and psychiatric disorders. A qualitative research design was utilized, employing a snowball sample of 12 substance abuse counselors with graduate degrees in counseling and other related fields. Substance abuse counselors were those who are currently working in substance abuse treatment programs serving clients with dual disorders. Personal interviews were conducted in a standardized open-ended approach with the 12 substance abuse counselors. Each counselor participated in two interviews in their natural setting (e.g. home and/or office). Fourteen common themes emerged from the counselors' responses. Ten themes (treatment services, availability of community resources, assessment/diagnosis of dual disorders, psychiatric disorders, specific treatment team members, types of MISA professional training preparation, helpful professional training experiences, professional training limitations, recommendations for future MISA professional training and miscellaneous) were central to the professional training experiences of the substance abuse counselors working with clients with MISA. Although, the remaining four themes (needs of clients with dual disorders, increasing compliance among clients with MISA, working on a multidisciplinary team, and on-the-job training experience) were not central to all the counselors, they emerged as essential for enough of the substance abuse counselors' interviewed to be also considered vital information relevant to their work with clients with dual disorders. Substance abuse counselors revealed that treatment services included individual and group counseling, case management, medication management, psychological testing, and community referral. Counselors indicated that clients with dual disorders possessed mood, anxiety, personality, and adolescent disorders. Most substance abuse counselors in the study indicated that in-services and on-the-job training was most helpful in their preparation to work with clients with dual disorders. Counselors revealed that in-service and on-job-training made them more effective in working with other treatment team members. Other issues unrelated to the research questions emerged as a result of the personal interviews; these issues were also discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
