AB  - Research Objectives: Using data from the ACL National Evidence-Based Falls Prevention Database, investigate the outcomes of Georgia participants in the Matter of Balance (MoB) program, including stratified subgroups.DesignSecondary analysis of survey data including stratification based on self-reports of health conditions and demographic characteristics. Setting(s): General community, primarily senior centers. Participant(s): 405 participants (84% female, mean age 76) attending workshops at 28 sites between 3/30/2016 and 3/1/2017. Intervention(s): MoB is an eight-week group intervention (2 hours per meeting) targeting seniors at risk of falling. Trainers use cognitive-behavioral techniques to reduce the participants' fear of falling and encourage them to increase physical activity. Expected outcomes of the program are: reduced fear of falling, increased physical activity, increased strength, improved steadiness, confidence to get up after falling, and confidence to protect self if falling.Main Outcome Measure(s)The participants reported significant improvements on all measures except number of falls with injury and fear. Result(s): Participants who identified as having arthritis noted the most improvements (8 of 10 items), followed by participants with depression (5 of 10 items). Males, African Americans, and participants with limited activity and chronic health conditions entered the program with lower scores and reported more significant improvements across most measures than their counterparts. Conclusion(s): While the data set is limited by missing data and the fact that all data are self-reported, there are some significant conclusions to draw from this analysis. Older adults with comorbidities (e.g.; arthritis, depression diabetes, diabetes) and certain demographic characteristics (male, African-American, limited activity) may experience greater benefits than older adults participating in the program who do not identify as such. Further exploration is warranted to ensure that evidence-based community programs are addressing the wide-range of needs of older adults.
