AB  - INTERVENTION: Active Comparator Behavioral,Active Comparator Behavioral Determining the effect of follow‐up care model on self‐efficacy and readmission due to infection of patients with pemphigus. ,Determination and comparison of readmission due to infection of pemphigus patients before and after the intervention in the experimental and control groups. This study is a clinical trial study with an experimental group and a control group, in which the effect of implementing a follow‐up care model on self‐efficacy and readmission due to infection of patients with pemphigus vulgaris referred to the clinic of Dermatology Hospital in Tehran . According to the entry and exit criteria and according to the sample size; Sampling will use the blocked random allocation method. The demographic questionnaire and the CDSES chronic disease self‐efficacy questionnaire will be completed initially for both intervention and control groups and will be completed again at the end of the 12th week after the intervention in both groups. It should be noted that the intervention group, in addition to the usual training based on the follow‐up care model, receives counseling and telephone follow‐ups through WhatsApp for 3 months. The researcher uses a follow‐up care model that includes: familiarization, sensitization, control and evaluation steps. In the familiarization phase, care problems are identified and reviewed, and the researcher holds a 15‐ to 45‐minute session with the patient for this purpose. In the second stage, continuous training is provided in the form of individual counseling sessions or in the form of a three‐ to five‐person group discussion on complication control and quality of life improvement, which lasts an average of 4 to 6 sessions with patients. Due to the pandemic conditions, all sessions are performed with the full consent of the patient and the minimum number of patients and the observance of standard precautions and social distance. In the control phase, the follow‐up of issues is done weekly and by phone and through WhatsApp application, and sometimes during each patient visit to the clinic, in person, in accordance CONDITION: Because nurses play an important role in the care of pemphigus patients, and given the inherent complexities of today&#039;s health care system, it is the nurses&#039; challenge to demonstrate their interdisciplinary competence. Skin problems are one of the most common problems that nurses face. Pemphigus is a chronic and resistant disease that affects the self‐efficacy of the patient&#039;s life. Patients, on the other hand, find the disease to be significantly more crippling than what doctors say. The most important clinical goal in chronic diseases such as pemphigus is to increase self‐efficacy and prevent infection and hospitalization. Nurses, as the largest professional group in the health care system, can provide follow‐up treatment, reduce the number and frequency of hospitalizations, as well as reduce treatment and hospital costs; In fact, the patient and his family need follow‐up care and education, the use of nursing models is one of the most important and fundamental steps to achieve this goal. One of the proposed models is follow‐up care, continuous or continuous care is a regular process to establish effective, interactive and consistent communication between the client and the nurse, as a provider of health care services, in order to identify the needs and problems and sensitize clients to accept behaviors. Continuous hygiene and helping to maintain their recovery and improve their health. Therefore, considering the above issues and work experience in the dermatology department and close contact with these patients, also considering the chronicity of the disease and the need to continue to pay attention to controlling and preventing complications, the researcher decided to determine the effect of model implementation. Carry out follow‐up care for self‐efficacy and readmission due to infection in patients with pemphigus. It is expected that by presenting the results of this study to hospital officials and nurses as an important member of follow‐up car implementing follow‐up care model ; self‐efficacy; readmission due to infection; pemphigus vulgaris. PRIMARY OUTCOME: Determining and comparing the self‐efficacy of pemphigus patients before and after the intervention in the experimental and control groups. 3 month after intervention Demographic and chronic self‐efficacy questionnaire SECONDARY OUTCOME: Determination and comparison of readmission due to infection of pemphigus patients before and after the intervention in the experimental and control groups. 3 month after intervention Demographic and chronic self‐efficacy questionnaire INCLUSION CRITERIA: Inclusion criteria are: having pemphigus vulgaris that has been approved by the relevant doctor and 6 months have passed since the disease ‐ the patient is aware of his disease ‐ people between 18 to 60 years, alertness, knowledge and full understanding of the Persian language Absence of any mental illness, absence of other chronic diseases affecting the course of the disease (cardiovascular, cancer, etc.) ‐Lite literacy, having complete hearing, the ability to understand the training given to the patient .
