AB  - Geriatric depression is a social problem of great importance. Society is aging, and issues relating to mental health with this population will continue to become increasingly important. Mental health problems are closely related to negative outcomes in many other life domains for this oppressed and vulnerable population. This is especially true for the institutionalized elderly. Effective treatment modalities are lacking in general and are not always practical or appropriate for the geriatric population. The narrative approach, within an existential framework, was considered as a possible therapeutic process well suited for the institutionalized elderly in this study. An 8-week group intervention was conducted in a long-term care facility to study the impact of this approach using instruments to measure depression, self-esteem and meaning in life. In addition, the qualitative experience of group participation was examined through the use of a semi-structured interview. While the quantitative results did not demonstrate any statistically significant differences, the qualitative data demonstrated the usefulness of this type of intervention for the development of social support and better quality interpersonal relationships in institutional settings. A model was created to demonstrate how the process of higher quality social support developed throughout the group. Implications for social work practice are numerous and relate to all levels (micro, mezzo, and macro) of practice. Further research is necessary to develop specific practices to bolster social support in institutional settings. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
