AB  - Background: The recent literature suggests a changing paradigm in depression research: from a more categorical to a more dimensional approach, taking better into account the balance between negative and positive affect, and looking more carefully at anhedonia as well as cognitive function and functioning. [1, 2] Nevertheless, traditional scales used for assessment of the antidepressant efficacy are mainly focused on the negative affect. The LAPS (Leuven Affect and Pleasure Scale) has been created to measure, among others, the initially mentioned dimensions. Recent studies have shown a differential prof ile of Agomelatine in the improvement of positive affect, anhedonia and recovery of functioning [3, 4]. Objective(s): To assess changes in the different LAPS dimen sions (negative affect, positive affect, hedonic function, cognitive functioning, overall functioning, meaningfulness of life and happiness) in patients treated with Agomelatine. Method(s): A record of the clinical practice was made, using as population outpatient patients of psychiatric consultation in different attention centers in Colombia. Criteria for patient inclusion was set as patients 18 and older with a diagnosis of major depressive disorder in accordance to the DSM-5 criteria, who attend outpatient consultation, to whom the psychiatrist has started treatment with agomelatine and who have agreed to participate in the registry. Inclusion and treatment followed the contraindications, warnings and precautions required by the SmPC, including periodic liver monitoring. Participant physicians applied the LAPS questionnaire in at least in two visits (basal and eight weeks after treatment) to depressed patients, to assess the evolution in dimensions of depression. Result(s): 214 patients were assessed. 39% were men and 61% women; average age was 44 years old. In 59% of patients' diagnosis corresponded to the first episode and in 41% they had had more than one depressive episode. 8.9% of patients reported medical comorbidity, 7.0% reported a second diagnosis from psychiatry. Significant improvement was gained in all dimen sions of depression measured by LAPS. The score of negative affect passed from 6.9 to 3.0 (p < 0.05), positive affect passed from 3.4 to 7.0 (p < 0.5), hedonic function passed from 3.9 to 7.4 (p < 0.05), cognitive functioning from 4.2 to 7.6 (p < 0.05), overall functioning 4.1 to 7.7 (p < 0.05), meaningfulness of life from 4.2 to 7.7 (p < 0.05) and happiness moved from 3.3 to 7.3 (p < 0.05). An analysis was made by sex, age subgroups and if they corresponded to the first depression episode or not, maintaining a significant improvement in all dimensions measured by LAPS. Regarding treatment with agomelatine: no increase in transaminases (> 3 Upper Limit Normal) or other side effects causing an interruption of treatment was reported. Conclusion(s): (1) The use of the LAPS invites physicians to better assess the patient's perspective on depression, taking better into account the balance between negative and positive affect and the evolution in different dimensions to the habitual measured in the clinical practice. (2) Treatment with agomelatine resulted in significant changes on the different LAPS scores and the analysis in subgroups (gender, age, first or recurrent episode) gave better insight in the heterogeneity of depression.
