AB  - The current mental health system is failing to treat chronic mental illness successfully. The abandonment of long-term inpatient treatment led to conditions in which the mentally ill lived in the worst parts of communities, or simply were repeatedly admitted to mental hospitals. Problems to be dealt with include patients' tendencies toward dependency, their lack of living skills, & their tendency to be passive & to drop out of programs aimed at meeting their needs. An experimental training program was conducted with 65 adult patients in Dane County, Wisc, with a control sample of 65 patients. Work with patients aimed at developing independence & regular habits, & at giving reassurance; work with families aimed to break pathological dependency ties; work with the community aimed to develop collaboration & to make staff constantly available to help with problems & provide informal models for response to patients. The entire staff assumed joint responsibility for all patients. Experimental patients earned more money & had more social contacts, as well as having higher self-esteem & less symptomatology. Social costs were no higher; added economic benefits were higher than added costs. W. H. Stoddard.
