AB  - The purpose of the current study is to examine outcomes of reciprocal intimate partner violence among 199 homeless young adults in the Midwestern United States. Young people were interviewed on the streets and in shelters by outreach workers in youth service agencies. Results revealed that homeless youth who were victimized by their partner more often were significantly more likely to have traded sex, to have been sexually victimized on the street by someone other than an intimate partner, to have higher levels of illicit drug use, to have thought about suicide, to have engaged in numerous forms of self-mutilation, and to have higher levels of depressive symptoms and post traumatic stress symptoms. Additionally, reciprocal violence by homeless youth (i.e., those who admitted to victimizing their partners) was also significantly associated with many of the same high-risk outcomes. The results suggest that reciprocal violence is prevalent among this high-risk group of youth. Early intervention programs are needed to break the cycle of abuse that many youth experience first within the family and then later with intimate partners. Without intervention, many of these young people are at risk for continued exploitation and re-victimization on the street from intimate partners and other individuals. ..PAT.-Unpublished Manuscript [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Conference Papers -- American Society of Criminology is the property of American Society of Criminology and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
