AB  - 61 new female inmates (mean age 26.8 yrs) of the North Carolina Correctional Center for Women were randomly assigned to 1 of 3 3-wk treatment conditions—a no-treatment control group, a support-only group, or a psychodidactic-intervention support group—to determine the most effective strategy for facilitating the entry of women into correctional settings. All Ss were pre- and posttested with an expectancy rating form, background information and prison adjustment questionnaires, the Beck Depression Inventory, and the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory. Findings reveal that the psychodidactic groups expected greater benefits from the orientation program than did the support-only group, but there were no significant differences between these 2 groups following treatment. However, both experimental groups did report a significant decrease in psychosomatic symptomatology compared with the control group, suggesting that the opportunity for Ss to discuss their feelings and share relevant information about their early days in prison was related to a decrease in the number of physical complaints. (13 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
