AB  - Examined the role of social support in moderating the impact of childhood sexual abuse on adult psychological adjustment. Ss included 475 women (aged 18–45 yrs) some of whom were currently receiving treatment, others who were not. Women in the treatment group were receiving therapy for either alcoholism, for being battered, or for mental health problems. Among both groups, Ss who had been sexually abused exhibited more psychological symptoms and lower self-esteem compared with Ss who were not abused. Among Ss in the comparison group, Ss who experienced supportive reactions following disclosure of sexual abuse had fewer psychological symptoms and somewhat higher self-esteem relative to those who did not receive support. Social support had no apparent effect on the long-term adjustment of Ss in the treatment group. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
