AB  - This paper reports the findings of a qualitative study designed to elicit the perceptions of cancer patient support group members attending an art group. A total of 25 patients with varying forms of cancer were individually interviewed on their perceptions of art therapy using semi-structured open-ended interview techniques. The participants were regular attendees at an art (painting) group organised through cancer patient support group meetings. The group meets one morning a week and each member can choose his/her subject to paint. The leader of the group is a qualified artist and group members have access to an oncology nurse.Recently, various manifestations of the arts have been employed in healthcare -- including mental health, paediatric health, and care for the chronically ill and for disabled patients -- as successful diversional and therapeutic interventions. This study found that art therapy contributed to the participants adopting a positive attitude to life and coping more effectively with cancer. The findings from this study challenge Australian healthcare systems to consider the benefits of providing art therapy as a complementary support mechanism to patients with cancer.
