AB  - Discusses the Soteria project, which compared short-term hospitalization and anti-psychotic drug treatment (control) with treatment in a small, home-like social environment, usually without neuroleptics (experimental condition). 100 Ss (aged 18–30 yrs) with schizophrenia newly diagnosed by the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-II (DSM-II) were randomly assigned to treatment in 2 experimental and 2 control settings. Treatment environments were studied by means of Community Oriented Program Environment (COPES) and Ward Atmosphere (WAS) scales. The groups were comparable on 25 of 29 admission variables. At 6 wks, psychopathology in both groups had improved significantly and overall change was the same. The specially designed, replicable milieus were able to reduce acute psychotic symptomatology within 6 wks, usually without antipsychotic drugs, and were as effective as usual hospital ward treatment that included routine neuroleptic drug use. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
