AB  - Discusses an open-ended, long-term group for borderline mothers (aged approximately 40–50 yrs) of adolescent alcoholics in terms of a 5-stage model of group development (J. A. Garland et al, 1965). Descriptions of the group at each stage (pre-affiliation, power and control, intimacy, differentiation, separation) illustrate how group process helped change the behavior and goals of the mothers, enabling them to separate from their children. A brief history of the group's background, presenting problems, and history is given. The leadership requirements, problems, and enabling strategies involved in working with this group are discussed. It is concluded that the most important leadership requirement was being able to form a strong therapeutic relationship with each client over a long period of time. Reasons for the group's longevity that illustrate its effectiveness are suggested. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
