AB  - A qualitative assessment of the use of music in brief (4 2-hr sessions) group work was conducted with 3 couples, 1 of each of whom was an identified alcoholic by virtue of their past completion of a residential program for alcoholics. The couples concurrently participated in an aftercare program and were also engaged in work with a mental health professional outside of this study's preview. This qualitative study made the following tentative inferences: (1) that such combined music-group work intervention will facilitate generalist problem-solving across psycho-social domains (e.g., alleviation of depression, fewer problems with personal relationships at home or at work); (2) an estimated 9 of every 10 clients are expected to experience such problem alleviation or amelioration; and (3) that, not surprisingly, the effectiveness of this intervention is specific to those clients (an estimated 80%) who reported that they enjoyed music. This study laid the theoretical groundwork for the coherent design of a music-group work intervention, the more methodologically rigorous evaluation of which is currently underway with a large quasi-experiment for outpatient clients. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
