AB  - The purpose of this study is to explore the way in which treatment and evaluation of problematic sexual behaviors requires modification in the presence of psychotic symptoms. Individuals who experience psychotic symptoms and express sexually problematic behaviors are thought to constitute less than five percent of those perpetrating sexual violence. While empirical literature acknowledges that a complex relationship exists between problematic sexual behaviors and psychosis, the nature of this relationship has yet to be explicitly defined. Furthermore, because the manner of the interaction between these variables is likely to differ on an individual basis, it may be difficult to identify one set of treatment interventions that best serves this small, but important, subset of severely mentally ill individuals. This is further complicated by the fact that current sexual offender treatment methodologies have largely been designed without consideration of psychotic symptoms and because many research studies on sexually offending populations have specifically excluded participants with histories of severe mental illness. Therefore, there is little empirical data speaking to the treatment interventions best equipped to reduce rates of sexually violent recidivism among individuals with co-morbid psychotic symptoms. In order to develop recommendations as to how current treatment methodologies and evaluative techniques may be best tailored for individuals with co-morbid psychosis and problematic sexual behavior, an online survey was distributed to clinicians and evaluators working for the Massachusetts' Department of Mental Health's Mentally Ill/Problematic Sexual Behavior Program. The qualitative survey aimed to gather both demographic information regarding the client population as well as clinical opinions of various factors related to psychotic symptomatology which impact treatment delivery and risk evaluation. The results indicated that many aspects of traditional sexual offender treatment, including group therapy, cognitive-behavioral techniques and social skills training can be successfully modified for clients who exhibit psychotic symptomatology. However, there a number of alternative techniques including, individual treatment and behavioral interventions, that may be more appropriate due to a number of factors. Furthermore, the study also demonstrates that more research is needed in the area of risk evaluation to ensure that risk predictions appropriately reflect all relevant psychiatric and criminogenic factors contributing to an individual's problematic sexual behavior. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
