AB  - Purpose: Chronic neck pain (CNP) is a major problem in the UK, affecting up to 17.9% of the population. Patients with CNP are frequently referred to physiotherapy, however, previous studies suggest that current physiotherapy treatments often result in only moderate improvements in pain and disability. Consequently, alternative treatment strategies are required to improve patient outcomes. Previous work suggests that certain cognitive factors, such as catastrophizing and self‐efficacy beliefs, are strongly related to levels of pain and disability in this population. However, it is unclear whether targeting these cognitive factors improves patient outcome. Therefore, this study compared a physiotherapist‐led pain management programme, designed to modify cognitive factors, to a conventional physiotherapy exercise regime. Relevance: Establishing whether modifying cognitive factors improves clinical outcome will allow physiotherapists to offer treatments which are most likely to benefit patients with CNP. Participants: All patients with non‐specific neck pain of at least three months duration who were referred to four UK National Health Service out‐patient physiotherapy departments were invited to participate. One hundred and nine patients gave informed consent, of which 57 satisfied the inclusion criteria. The main reasons for exclusion were failure to attend assessment (n = 11), radicular symptoms (n = 9), vertebral artery insufficiency (n = 7) and rheumatological disorder (n = 5). Methods: Patients were contacted by post and invited to participate. Consenting patients completed a questionnaire prior to assessment. Those satisfying the inclusion criteria were randomised to receive a Progressive Neck Exercise Programme (PNEP) or Interactive Behavioural Modification Therapy (IBMT). IBMT is a physiotherapist‐led group‐based intervention. It is underpinned by cognitive behavioural principles and has previously been shown to reduce disability in patients with chronic low back pain. It aims to reduce catastrophizing and enhance self‐efficacy beliefs through interactive education, condition specific exercise and progressive functional goal setting. PNEP consisted of a programme of cervical flexibility and strengthening exercises, which were progressively increased in difficulty. Follow up data were collected after six months by postal questionnaire. Analysis: Independent t‐tests were performed to establish whether significant differences existed in mean reductions in pain and disability between the two groups. The number of patients making a minimal clinically important change (MCIC) in pain and disability was also calculated and chisquared tests performed to establish whether this differed significantly between the groups. Results: There was no significant difference in mean reduction in disability between the groups (difference = 2.8, p = 0.45), however, a significantly greater number of participants made a MCIC in disability in the IBMT group (59% vs. 25%). Moreover, participants receiving IBMT reported greater mean reductions in pain (difference = 1.1, p = 0.05) and a significantly greater proportion reported a MCIC in pain than with PNEP (55% vs. 25%). Conclusions: In patients with CNP, IBMT resulted in superior clinical outcomes when compared to PNEP. Specifically, participants were more likely to experience a clinically significant reduction in pain and disability and reported greater mean reductions in levels of pain. Implications: When treating patients with CNP physiotherapists should offer a structured exercise programme in combination with treatment aiming to modify specific cognitive factors. Treatment should incorporate group‐based interactive education sessions and progressive, functional goal setting exercises.
