AB  - Background and Objective(s): Evidence suggests adults with cerebral palsy (CP) underutilize specialty and ancillary services. Musculoskeletal (MSK) disorders pose a great risk to accelerating functional and biological decline with aging and increase the risk of comorbidities. More information is needed to evaluate if disparities exist among adults with CP and non-CP peers with similar MSK disorders in utilization of services and comorbidities. Among older adults with specific MSK diagnoses and CP, we evaluated the effect of CP on use of and number of visits to physical (PT) and occupational therapy (OT) and on presence of comorbidities. Study Design: Retrospective, observational, population based. Study Participants and Settings: Community-living older adults, 65-99 years, with CP who had >24 months of continuous enrollment in Medicare between 2011 and 2014 and sought ambulatory care for one or more MSK conditions (N=56 311 person-years) and a matched non-CP cohort (N=112 622 person-years) with similar demographic and clinical characteristics. Material(s) and Method(s): A 20% national sample of Medicare claims data (2011-2014) identified adults with CP (ICD-9-CM diagnosis codes: 3430-31, 3433-34, 3438-39, 33 371) who sought ambulatory care for MSK condition(s). Frequencies of diagnosis subgroups were compared to non-CP peers. Adults with CP with MSK >65 years were matched with a non-CP cohort on MSK diagnosis, age, gender, race, and census region. Use of PT and OT services were identified via CPT and revenue center codes. Descriptive statistics compared therapy use/visits, and the prevalence of comorbidities (Elixhauser Index). Regression analyses evaluated the effect of CP diagnosis on therapy use, number visits, and the prevalence of comorbidities in the subgroups of adults with soft tissue or joint pain MSK diagnoses, controlling for demographic and clinical factors. Result(s): Diagnostic codes identified 33 849 adults with CP with MSK and 6 742 954 adults with MSK. MSK diagnostic subgroup frequencies were similar among both groups except adults with CP had more joint derangement and spinal curvature (Table 1). Adults with CP>65 who sought care for MSK disorders (N=7730) were 53% female, 82% White, with a median age of 72 years. Frequency of PT use was lower among adults with CP (19%) as compared to their matched peers (28%). Average PT visits for adults with CP and MSK disorders were less (7.8, SD 11.6) than peers (9.4, SD 11.2). Adults without CP and either soft tissue disorders or joint pain were 1.16 (1.08, 1.25) and 1.27 (1.16, 1.38) more likely to use PT services relative to CP peers (Table 2). Frequency of OT use and mean visits were similar among adults with and without CP (6% use for CP vs 4% non-CP; mean visits 4.7 for CP vs 5.0 for non-CP). Mean number of comorbidities for adults with CP was 5.6 (SD 4.3) compared to 3.2 (SD 3.1) for peers (Table 2). For adults with CP with either soft tissue disorders or joint pain there was an increased likelihood (odds ratios 1.13-33.0) of having the following: other neurological disorders, psychoses, weight loss, congestive heart failure, deficiency anemias, depression, paralysis, and peripheral vascular disease (Table 3). Conclusions or Significance: Less than a third of older adults without CP and MSK disorders, and even fewer adults with CP and MSK disorders utilize rehabilitation services. Older adults with CP and MSK disorders are at greater risk than their peers for secondary conditions that significantly impact morbidity, mortality, and quality of life. Adults with CP and MSK disorders would likely benefit from increased utilization of rehabilitation services.
