AB  - Background: Despite having attracted the attention of researchers for years, the effects of amphetamine on male sexual function remains poorly characterized. Stimulants are reported to reverse a variety of sexual side effects caused by SSRIs or MAOIs. While some patients report enhanced sexual desire, spontaneous erections upon use, some patients report delayed or multiple orgasms, reduced libido, impaired ejaculation, and difficulty achieving orgasm secondary to a stimulant abuse. Method(s): We describe a 30-year-old man with long-standing attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder and a three year history of misuse of stimulants (e. g., Adderall 100 mg daily or 150- 200 mg two-three days a week, rarely in conjunction with up to one gram of cocaine) presenting for evaluation of sexual complaints leading to severe depression with suicidal ideation. He had previously taken methylphenidate and dextroamphetamine in therapeutic doses without sexual side effects. He presented for an outpatient consultation with Endocrinology to evaluate a 12-month history of sexual dysfunction (inability to obtain/maintain erection, anorgasmia), and a belief that his genitals were shrinking. Symptoms began immediately after taking Adderall 100 mg, leading to sexual dysfunction and avoidance of partnered sexual activity. Prior to starting Adderall, he had a very active sexual life, but after onset of sexual dysfunction his mood worsened, and one week prior to evaluation, he attempted suicide via polydrug overdose. During his evaluation, he noted that he would accept physician-assisted suicide were it offered. He was evaluated by multiple services, including Endocrinology, Urology, Neurology, Addiction Psychiatry, Adult General Psychiatry, Psychology, Dermatology, Vascular Surgery, Internal Medicine, Rheumatology, Complementary and Integrative Medicine and Gastroenterology. An extensive medical evaluation was unrevealing. Unremarkable results included CBC, electrolytes, coagulation tests, ESR, vitamin B1, B2, B6, B12 levels, liver and kidney function tests, prostate-specific antigen, A. C. E., lipid panel, cryoglobulin, cryofibrinogen, TSH, testosterone, beta-HCG, DHT, prolactin, LH, FSH, estradiol, complement C1q, C3, C4, rheumatoid factor, antihistone, CRP, myeloperoxidase, proteinase, antinuclear antibodies, phospholipids/cardiolipin antibodies, bacteria/rickettsia microbiology, fungi, viral hepatitis screen, HIV, parasites screen, serum zinc, urinalysis, semen analysis, semen morphology, CSF autoimmune dementia panel, testicular ultrasound, EEG, brain MRI, head MRA and a Doppler waveform of his bilateral arms. The lone remarkable result was low ferritin [8 ng/ml (normal: 24-336 ng/ml)]. Penile injection of papaverine, phentolamine, and alprostadil resulted in a weak erection. Penile ultrasound demonstrated venous leak. Neuropsychological assessment revealed mild impairment in non-verbal reasoning and problem solving. His erectile dysfunction was determined to be multifactorial, with the main etiological factor being the direct side effect of Adderall (possibly secondary to moderate serotonergic effects of methylphenidate and amphetamine). Other contributors included anxiety and depression as a result of his sexual dysfunction, as well as a spectator phenomenon related to previous episodes of erectile dysfunction. Our patient demonstrates persistent sexual side effects and cognitive decline (weakness in non-verbal reasoning and problem solving) in the context of supratherapeutic Adderall doses, and reminds the clinician to be cautious when prescribing stimulants for sexual side effects, and to consider the potential for sexual and cognitive side effects, especially when exceeding the FDA maximum doses. Result(s): Stimulants are believed to enhance sex drive, lower inhibitions, and improve sexual performance. They are used to counteract sexual side effects from antidepressants. Stimulants can also cause severe sexual dysfunction. Conclusion(s): When prescribing a stimulant for sexual side effects of antidepressants, one must be aware of that this class of medications may enhance sexual function, or may cause impotence. Summary: Psychostimulants may reverse sexual dysfunction secondary to antidepressants or when used for recreational purposes, or may cause sexual dysfunction.
