AB  - The three books reviewed here are recent additions to the up-and-coming field of disability studies. They admirably illustrate how the exploration of disability is striving to advance up the policy agenda of welfare states as well as the research and theoretical terrains of the humanities and social sciences. All three works make clear how the seemingly particular and individual troubles of chronic illness and physical or mental impairments are properly understood as public issues of social exclusion, discrimination and oppression. "Disability," as Barnes, Oliver and [Len Barton] state in their reader (2002: 2), "is both a common personal experience and a global phenomenon, with widespread economic, cultural and political implications for society as a whole." Titchkosky asserts that, "It is impossible to experience disability outside of our relations with others. Whole cultures and whole societies experience disability" (2003: 4-5). A paradox exists, however, in that while the sociological imagination is a prominent feature of the field of disability studies, this newly emerging field has a troubled relationship with mainstream sociology. Disability, Self, and Society by Tanya Titchkosky, Associate Professor of Sociology at St. Francis Xavier University, "attends to the cultural processes of meaning-making surrounding disability" (2003: 4). As a study set methodologically in phenomenology, Titchkosky explores the lived experience and meanings of her own dyslexia, and the blindness of her partner, Rod Michalko, a fellow sociologist who is blind. Adopting the view that culture is interactive, both giving and receiving meaning from people, Titchkosky examines disability as social phenomenon and identity, and as a place for critical inquiry into the knowledge and conceptions of society. Disability, Self, and Society is an engaging, thought provoking blend of the intimate personal biography and the wider theoretical issues of culture and society. Disability studies take a critical stance toward traditional approaches to researching and practice on impariment. The field challenges orthodox explanations of disability in individual and medical frameworks, and traditional sociological treatments of disability as a form of deviance from the normal and hence a spoiled identity -- what British writers in the field frequently call the "personal tragedy" model of disability. The core theoretical paradigm of the field is a social model of disability, reflecting both the politicization of disability by disabled people and groups, and the strong sociological roots of the academic field. In this light, disability is socially created and politically mediated, resulting all too often in experiences of exclusion and oppression.
