AB  - Investigated the influence of a nursing intervention to instill hope on levels of hope, self-efficacy, self-esteem, and depression with 40 homeless veterans. Ss completed the Miller Hope Scale, Self-Efficacy Scale, Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale, and the Beck Depression Inventory. Support for the homelessness–hopelessness theory was found, as pre-intervention Ss showed a high level of depression and low levels of hope, self-efficacy, and self-esteem. Levels of hope and self-esteem increased and depression decreased in Ss who received the nursing intervention. Treatment and control groups differed significantly with regard to hope at posttest. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
