AB  - Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) attendance is predictive of drinking reduction for many problem drinkers (Moos and Moos, 2006; Kelly et al., 2007), and treatment providers routinely refer patients to AA. While therapeutic strategies to initiate AA attendance are effective (e.g., Nowinski, 2006), rates of AA attendance after treatment typically follow a quadratic pattern wherein immediate increases in AA meeting attendance are followed by rapid decay over a 6-month period. It is not clear, however, if this pattern of AA attendance is specific to treatment-referred alcoholics or if it represents a more general pattern of AA affiliation. Further, the AA specific and non-specific factors that may moderate this pattern of AA attendance are rarely investigated. This study (R01AA014197, Tonigan, PI) addressed these questions by applying longitudinal hierarchical linear modeling (HLM) in a sample of alcoholics recruited from community-based AA (n = 70) and treatment centers (n = 140). AA meeting attendance was measured using the Form 90 (Miller, 1996), a reliable calendar-based approach that yields percent days of AA attendance in 3-month increments, i.e., baseline, 3 and 6-month follow-ups. Consistent with earlier reports, findings suggested that an overall quadratic growth rate accounted for 31% of the variability in individual growth rates of AA attendance from intake to the 6-month follow-up. Inclusion of recruitment source (level 2), however, indicated that adults recruited from community-based AA initially affiliated via meeting attendance at twice the rate of treatment seeking adults ($=.49, p <.001), but that the decay in AA attendance from the 3 to 6-month follow-up was significantly more rapid for the community-based sample ($= -.21, p <.001). Finally, acquiring a sponsor (level 2) did not appear to moderate initial increases in AA attendance, but having a sponsor did significantly slow the decay in rates of AA attendance between the 3 and 6-month follow-up. This study offers a rare view to judge the long-term effectiveness of treatment providers to engage alcoholics into AA. Clinical implications are discussed.
