AB  - Objectives: The successful implementation of a trauma informed curriculum (TIC) must highlight physical, psychological, and emotional safety for patients, students, staff, and faculty. Survivors of emotionally harmful experiences, such as violence, abuse, neglect, disaster, or loss, represent 56% of the population. To secure positive outcomes, it is imperative that we groom the next generation of trauma sensitive dentists through the education of stakeholders at all levels. Method(s): Rutgers School of Dental Medicine (RSDM) collaborated with Cicatelli Associates, Inc. (CAI) to build a trauma sensitive health care delivery environment and culture. The Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration's (SAMHSA's) trauma-informed approach was the roadmap to implement TIC in dental education. Key to this method is the Four R concept: Realizing the widespread impact and prevalence of trauma and pathways to recovery; Recognizing the signs and symptoms of trauma; Responding by integrating this knowledge into curriculum, policies, procedures, and practices; andResisting re-traumatization. Result(s): RSDM developed a trauma-informed culture by subscribing to SAMHSA's six key principles of TIC: safety, trustworthiness, peer support, collaboration, empowerment, and cultural/gender awareness. With these overarching tenets in mind, RSDM and CAI implemented: leadership trainings; faculty, staff, and student education sessions; cultural and physical environment assessments; de-escalation trainings; an RSDM TIC task force; patient, staff, and student centered initiatives; faculty and staff focus groups; and student/new employee trauma orientation sessions. Conclusion(s): A trauma-informed organization demonstrates a commitment to empathy and effective practices. It is responsible to make dental students, faculty, and staff aware of the widespread nature of trauma and prepare them to manage its effects in a secure and considerate manner to improve health outcomes. As the concept of a trauma-informed approach becomes a central focus in multiple service sectors, dental schools must promote a shared support of TIC.
