AB  - This paper investigates injecting, shared use of needles/syringes and sexual risk behaviors at intake to treatment and at one-year follow-up among 753 drug users recruited to the National Treatment Outcome Research Study (NTORS). Injecting, sharing and having unprotected sex were substantially reduced among clients admitted to methadone programs and among those admitted to residential treatments. The overall levels of risk fell after treatment, and the majority of those who were engaged in high risk behaviors at intake had stopped at follow-up. The results also show the variability of individual outcomes. A minority persisted with their risk behavior, and others who were not at risk at intake who had started to engage in risky behaviors at follow-up. The behavior of these clients creates a focal point for risk as well as being a threat to public health. Several social and psychological factors were predictive of health risk behaviors. These included frequency and duration of heroin use, polydrug use, alcohol use, gender, ethnicity, having a drug-using partner, anxiety and depression. The results indicate the important role that can be played by treatment services in helping to reduce the risk of blood-borne infections. We suggest that risk reduction interventions are an important and effective component of treatment programs.
