AB  - A critical review of recent res on the etiology of alcoholism. Theories include: (1) Physiological Deficiencies (poor nutrition, genetic factors), & (2) Psychol'al-Emotional Maladjustments. In both theories the deficiency or maladjustment has not been established prior to alcoholism nor, even if this point is granted, have these theories established how these defects are translated into alcoholism or why all people with these defects do not become alcoholics. (3) sociol'al & anthrop'al theory stresses lack of effective controls, as in explaining high alcoholism rates among the Irish where those with physiological or psychol'al deficiencies find alcohol defined as the way to manage individual tension, in contrast to Orthodox Jews who also consume alcohol but have low alcoholism rates because they link alcohol to moral symbolism & religious practices. The question remains: what is the specific process by which alcoholic predisposition is linked to alcohol use (& overuse) in a given soc environment. It is contended 'that the coupling often takes place through the status-reward present in face-to-face drinking groups. Within the reference points provided by these groups, predisposed persons are tutored to use alcohol excessively. Group rewards provide positive motivations to use alcohol. However, group exclusion also develops & the budding alcoholic seeks out new drinking groups that will continue to reward his drinking behavior. In this manner, he reaches groups where `loss of control' is the expected norm. A basic res problem in therapy lies in how to detect & reduce the barriers to affiliation with therapeutic groups such as Alcoholics Anonymous. The induction practices of a receiving AA group may act selectively to deter affiliation... .Or, on the other hand, affiliation motive [as measured by TAT] may be weak in those who cannot affiliate.' S. F. Fava.
