AB  - Background: Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) is a painful chronic gastrointestinal disease characterized by inflammation. Along with physical symptoms, patients with IBD report psychosocial distress associated with diminished quality of life (QoL). Cognitive‐behavioral therapy programs are effective in improving psychosocial distress and QoL in chronic pain conditions, but the few programs examined in IBD have had mixed results. Aims: To develop and test a patient driven, empirically supported group program for the IBD patient experience. This report examines weekly changes in the biopsychosocial risk factors on a pilot group of 9 participants (5 intervention group, 4 control group). Methods: The inclusion criteria included a score below 210 on the IBDQ‐36, and a score above 7 on at least one of the subscale (i.e., depression, anxiety) the HADS. Participants were excluded if they were presently having issues with drug or alcohol abuse, if they had a diagnosis of psychotic disorder or if they were presently already in psychotherapy. A total of 15 of 41 participants (12 women, 3 men) completed a screening questionnaire and met study inclusion criteria from the tertiary care clinics at Hotel Dieu hospital in Kingston. Participants were randomly assigned to the group therapy program or to an online psychoeducation reading group. Six participants were excluded for various reasons (i.e., did not return our calls, cannot commit to the program). The therapy program involved 8 weekly group workshops [60 mins] with 2 group facilitators addressing catastrophizing, social support, coping strategies, and embarrassment associated with IBD. The psychoeducation group involved weekly readings of 30 minutes on different IBD themes. All the participants completed weekly measures on depression, anxiety, catastrophizing, social support, body image, stigma, and shame. Results: All participants in the therapy group completed the program, and 3 participants included in the control dropped out of the study. Data was analyzed in an intent to treat protocol. A repeated measures ANOVA conducted to evaluate the pre‐post differences on psychosocial variables showed that depression was significantly impacted (p < .05). Depression scores decreased over time for participants in the intervention group and increased slightly for participants in the control group. Conclusions: These preliminary results indicate the promise of reducing depression over a short therapy engagement. Future analyses will examine changes in depression as mechanisms for changes in patient QoL over time. With a larger sample size, the discussion of generalizability and the potential effect of other psychosocial variables will be provided.
