AB  - Although 20% of U.S. college-aged young adults meet the criteria for an alcohol use disorder, only 4% received any form of treatment (Wu et al., 2007). One avenue to reduce the treatment underutilization is to understand decision processes involved in seeking and selecting treatment providers for alcohol issues. This study aims to examine the following questions: (a) what treatment provider-related information is sought when selecting a treatment option?, (b) what is the process of selecting treatment providers?; (c) what are the factors and information used when choosing a treatment provider for alcohol use issues? This study utilized the Think Aloud Protocol (TAP), which asks participants to verbalize their mental processes or "inner monologue" (or think aloud) as they make decisions. The TAP study was set in a laboratory setting wherein undergraduate participants (N = 20), with access to a computer and the internet, are tasked to search for alcohol abuse treatment that they would seek treatment from for their own hypothetical alcohol use issues. The following data were audio- and videorecorded and used in the analyses: (a) verbalizations of participants' mental processes (thinking aloud); (b) the websites they visited; and (c) a videorecording of the entire search procedure. A Grounded Theory (Strauss & Corbin, 1990) analytical frame was used to analyze the qualitative data. Qualitative analyses results indicated that naive participants generally start with very broad search using very broad search strings (e.g., "alcohol problem" or "alcohol help") which leads to frustration and feeling overwhelmed. Those with prior personal or family experience with alcoholism tend to use finer search parameters (e.g., searching for Alcoholics Anonymous). The most important information used in decision making was proximity of services. There was more reliance on website aesthetics and internet reviews (e.g., personal reviews and Google reviews' 5-star system) when making a treatment provider choice, and less reliance on actual treatment-related information (e.g., inpatient vs. outpatient). Results of this study provide the process/flow-chart of how college-aged individuals select a substance use counselor or treatment facility. The developed model outlines, in a progressive fashion, the decision-making process potential clients go through as they seek treatment.
