AB  - This study investigated whether small psycho-educational groups could provide an effective means of treatment for mothers in the reunification process. A psycho-educational group program was developed and implemented by WestCoast Children's Center. Each group session consisted of 12 weekly meetings, 1.5 hours in length, for 5 to 8 mothers over a period of 2 years. The psycho-educational model incorporated group processes such as engagement and support, and active skill-building such as conflict resolution, as well as information on childrearing issues and methods. Data was obtained for 20 of the 29 women enrolled in the program. Each participant was administered the Adult-Adolescent Parenting Inventory (AAPI-2), the Brief Symptom Inventory (BSI), the Parenting Stress Index (PSI) and the YIACT Parenting Attitudes Survey prior to group participation. Following the 12-week group, each participant was administered the above-mentioned measures, except the Brief Symptom Inventory, and with the addition of the Client Satisfaction Questionnaire (CSQ-8). Women who completed the pre-group evaluation process but failed to complete the post-group measures (n = 11) were compared to women who completed both pre- and post-group evaluations and attended all or most of the groups (n = 9). No significant differences were found between group completers and noncompleters. For completers, the hypothesis that the WestCoast psycho-educational group program would increase participants' knowledge and understanding of developmentally and age-appropriate behaviors for children, raise knowledge of appropriate and effective parenting skills and techniques, and increase understanding of the participants own internal processes with respect to her parenting, was largely supported by the results. A trend was shown towards development of a more realistic view of participants' competence as parents, as well as reduction in levels of depression and feelings of isolation and stress associated with parenting. On average, participants rated the program from 'good' to 'excellent'. Inclusion of fathers in treatment, concurrent treatment programs for children, and consideration of the impact of societal and systemic issues such as poverty, addiction, and poor education, were cited as issues for consideration in future programs and further study. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
