AB  - ; The article examines utilization of psychosocial treatments by patients diagnosed with bipolar disorder and substance dependence. It examines psychosocial treatment interventions, mood symptoms and substance use among the patients. It indicates that the most commonly used treatments were individual psychotherapy and Alcoholics Anonymous. It shows that these findings may be considered in light of suggestions that psychosocial interventions are thought to enhance bipolar patients' overall functioning. Even though sufficient number of clinical trials of the effectiveness are not available, preliminary studies conducted with bipolar patients with the substance use status unstated suggest that interventions such as group and family therapy and psychoeducation regarding bipolar disorder may effectively reduce functional morbidity. It was also found that most patients never made use of other psychosocial treatment modalities, such as family or group psychotherapy. Therefore, it appears that these patients underutilized currently recommended treatments.
