AB  - Counseling psychologists who opt to work with the increasing older population will benefit from holding on to core values of the profession as well as from being versed in applicable current research and theory (see Volume 1, Chapter 18, this handbook), clinical skills targeted for older adults (Knight et al., 2009), and the business aspects of providing clinical services to a later life population (Hartman-Stein, 2006). This chapter begins by focusing on clinical issues that are often experienced by older adults, including depression, anxiety, and substance abuse. Following is a discussion of individual and group psychotherapy, as well as pharmacological intervention, emphasizing evidence-based practice (EBP); areas for further exploration among evidence-based treatments for older adults are highlighted. Given the need for collaborative care with an older adult population, three additional areas of clinical intervention are explored, including assessment, prevention or promotion of health, and working within an interdisciplinary context. Next, cognitive impairment, pain management, and end-of-life issues—three areas that are emerging as significant issues for clinical practice with older adults—are explored. Attention is then turned to topics centered on business issues when working with older adults, recognizing the importance of Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) policies, particularly the central requirement for 'medical necessity.' Emphasis is given to the research needed to further the beneficial use of this concept. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
