T1  - Cervical Cancer Screening in the United States: Challenges and Potential Solutions for Underscreened Groups
JF  - OBSTETRICAL & GYNECOLOGICAL SURVEY
A1  - Van Le L
KW  - eppi-reviewer
PY  - 2021
DA  - 2021///
Y1  - 2021///
AB  - Every year, approximately 13,000 cases and 4000 deaths are attributed to cervical cancer, making it the leading cause of cancer death for women worldwide. Nearly all cases of cervical cancer are caused by human papillomavirus (HPV), making HPV vaccination and screening programs critical for reducing mortality. Although screening rates in the United States are greater than 80%, there are significant disparities with underscreened populations including racial or ethnic minorities, rural area residents, sexual or gender minorities, some religious groups, those with limited English proficiency, people with various health conditions including physical disabilities, and people with mental health concerns. The reasons behind these disparities are multifaceted and complex, including difficulty interacting with the health care system, financial barriers, and logistical concerns. This commentary article provides an overview of barriers leading to the underscreening of certain populations in the United States and proposes frameworks for improving these disparities. Rates of cervical cancer are higher for Black and Hispanic women compared with their non-Hispanic, White counterparts in the United States. Systemic racism interwoven in the health care industry leads to decreased adherence to screening protocols and low trust in providers. Potential interventions to address these disparities at their causes should include provider-, facility-, and system-level solutions including provider communication techniques aimed to established patient-centered relationships, patient navigation support, and "in reach" at nongynecologic visits to promote cervical cancer screening. Individuals with limited English proficiency demonstrate lower likelihood of receiving cervical cancer screening, and system-level solutions such as patient navigators and interpreters are critical for improving patient-centered care in this population. Certain religious groups with fatalistic beliefs and an emphasis on modesty are associated with lower rates of screening. In this population, community-level outreach by health care providers and systems to religious organizers and leaders are critical to address screening disparities. Individuals living in rural areas are less likely to complete cervical cancer screening and are diagnosed at later stages of disease compared with their counterparts in urban counties. Lack of access appears to be the key barrier in these areas, suggesting interventions such as mobile screening units, travel vouchers, and HPV self-sampling may be most effective. A recent national survey found that women not identifying as heterosexual were less likely to receive cervical cancer screening compared with those that identified as heterosexual. Transgender men also report particularly low rates of cervical cancer screening. Provider and facility-focused solutions aimed at minimizing stigmatizing language and care in addition to education interventions may be effective among this population. Individuals living with chronic comorbid conditions often have lower rate of cervical cancer screening despite their frequent interactions with the health care system, indicating provider-level interventions may be especially important here. Knowledge about cervical cancer, financial barriers especially among underinsured, and difficulty engaging in regular interactions with the health care system are key barriers that need addressing to reduce disparities regarding cervical cancer screening. Both community-based programs with the aim of increasing understanding and utilization of screening services and reminder programs wherein contact is initiated by the health care system have been shown to greatly increase screening rates. Insurance reform could address the disparities that exist among the underserved and improve rate of services from screening through treatment for cervical cancer. HPV self-sampling may simultaneously address several logistical barriers for underserved groups by increasing access and providing a convenient and private sampling process.
DO  - 10.1097/OGX.0000000000000927
