AB  - Background: Although pharmacotherapy forms the cornerstone of management in bipolar disorder, adjunctive psychosocial interventions are needed, especially to improve social and occupational functionality and quality of life. Psychosocial interventions such as psychoeducation also have been reported to have positive effects on clinical parameters such as prevention of episodes, decrease in hospitalization rates and prevention of suicides [1]. Since 2009, the Ministry of Health of Turkey began to put more focus on community-based mental health services and accordingly Community Mental Health Centers (CMHC) have been established in many regions and districts of the country. Community Mental Health Centers (CMHC) provide a comprehensive mental health service including psychoeducation, rehabilitation and home visits as well as the treatment and follow-up of psychiatric patients. The main target population of community-based mental health model consists of patients with severe mental disorders [2]. Therefore bipolar patients with more severe course, such as suffering from frequent and severe episodes, requiring frequent hospitalizations and experiencing significant loss in functionality, might be accepted to CMHCs. Aim(s): The aim of our study was to investigate the effect of CMHC services on clinical parameters in bipolar patients. Method(s): All bipolar patients registered to Bakirkoy CMHC in 2012 were included in the study. Along with sociodemographic data, clinical parameters such as compliance with treatment, relapses, hospitalizations, suicidal and homicidal behaviors and functionality were investigated in a 2-year period before application to CMHC and a 2-year period of follow-up in CMHC and compared. Result(s): In a total number of 43 patients, 25 (58%) were female, 18 (42%) were male and the mean age was 45.7+/-12.4 (range: 22-73) years. The majority was single (n = 24, 56%) and unemployed (n = 31, 72%). 39 (91%) patients received individual counseling and psychoeducation, 6 (14%) participated in group therapy and 12 (28%) in rehabilitation. Home visits were made in 26 (61%) patients. When 2-year periods before and in CMHC were compared, there was a significant difference in the rates of relapses, hospitalizations, suicidal and homicidal behaviors, compliance with treatment, forensic events and insight. No significant difference was obtained in professional functionality and alcoholsubstance abuse. Conclusion(s): Bipolar disorder is characterised with high relapse and hospitalization rates as well as negative consequences in professional and social functioning and quality of life. Noncompliance with treatment among patients with bipolar disorder is quite prevalent and possibly one of the essential causes of symptom worsening, low quality of life and even suicide in some cases [3,4]. Non-compliance with treatment is also associated with greater use of psychiatric emergency unit and hospitalizations [5]. CMHCs in the follow-up of bipolar patients, may help to achieve a good compliance with treatment, to prevent acute symptoms and to improve quality of life between episodes by providing the necessary environment for pharmacotherapy as well as psychosocial interventions.
