AB  - It is estimated that 8.2 million Americans alive today have a history of cancer (National Institute of Health, 1997). About 1,221,800 new cancer cases are expected to be diagnosed in 1999 (National Institute of Health, 1997), increasing the number of people who will be instantly placed in the role of caregiver to these cancer patients. Caregivers often become engulfed in this role, placing the needs of the care recipient before their own. The person designated into the role of caregiver often lacks the experience or understanding of all the responsibilities the role entails. Often the caregiver lacks self-confidence in their ability to perform their duties and question whether their efforts are enough. It is obvious that if the caregiver is suffering with any of these conditions, their effectiveness in completing the role of caregiver may be jeopardized and endangering the life of the care recipient and further adding to their feelings of guilt. The literature reveals caregivers experience strain as a result of the responsibilities involved in the role and suffer from stress, anxiety and depression. Extensive literature review reveals the lack of available treatment programs for caregivers of homebound cancer patients who are unable to seek treatment outside their home. The purpose of this dissertation was to create a treatment program based on the needs of this population, which could maximize utilization of the service. The program will be funded by grants from federal, state and local agencies as well as donations from community business, hospitals, and cancer organizations. The program's main office will be located near cancer treatment centers in an area predominantly Hispanic as this will be the target population. The program will utilize several treatment components such as individual and group therapy through home visits and use of technology; internet and teleconferencing. Cognitive-Behavior Therapy will be the treatment modality. Cognitive theories focus on the client's self-distortions which contribute to feelings of depression and anxiety and assist clients to alleviate symptoms by developing more flexible schemes and cognitive-behavioral responses. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
