AB  - Studied the relationship between length of hospital stay (LOS) and psychiatric consultation diagnosis of dysthymia or major depression among 48 inpatients (aged 19–85 yrs) hospitalized for medical or surgical reasons. Depressed Ss were matched with nondepressed Ss in the same medical/surgical diagnostic related group and were assessed during treatment and at follow-up. Results show that the mean LOS for depressed Ss was 20 days while the mean LOS for the control group was 10 days. Discussion focuses on the financial benefits to both the patient and the hospital of reduced LOS resulting from incorporating psychiatric treatment of depression in medical and surgical patients. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
