AB  - Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome (NAS) is a narcotic withdrawal disorder affecting digestive, breathing, and nervous systems of newborn babies. As opiate drug use has increased in the U.S., the rate and number of newborns suffering from NAS has also been on the rise. The majority of pregnancies by opiate using women are unintended. Also, once a pregnant woman has been identified as addicted to opiates, she is often treated with opioid agonists: methadone or buprenorphine. Studies have shown that pregnant women treated with opioid agonists may have reduced fetal mortality and improved fetal growth compared to heroin or pain-killers, but not reduced rates of NAS. The purpose of this study is to identify barriers and facilitators to effective family planning practices and tapering or detoxing, from the perspective of postpartum women in treatment. Postpartum women in treatment were recruited to participate in one of three focus groups, from a maternal and child home visitation program, and a large substance abuse treatment center, located in Pinellas County, Florida. A survey was also administered at the beginning of the two-hour focus group. Each participant was offered taxi and babysitting services, in addition to a $25 dollar gift card. The focus groups were facilitated and results analyzed by researchers trained and experienced in qualitative methods. There were 20 women that participated in the focus groups, where 90% were White and non-Hispanic with an average age of 30 years. At the time of their most recent pregnancy, 85% were not using birth control. Participants were not convinced of contraception effectiveness and confused about whether or not one could become pregnant while using illicit opiates. The cost of birth control was also cited as a common barrier. Almost all women were not in treatment prior to pregnancy (90%) and the range in current methadone dose was 15-350 mg. Most women (80%) were interested in tapering/detoxing, although a primary barrier was fear related to withdrawal sickness and relapse. The women indicated concern about the lack of child care resources that would be needed if they were to taper or detox. There was also a common belief that they had little control over their own treatment and they had concern over the lack of treatment options. The perspective of postpartum women enrolled in opiate treatment programs can provide insights into potential approaches to the prevention of NAS. Patient education, improved patient-provider communication and integration of primary care with substance abuse treatment, particularly for women of child-bearing age, could prevent NAS births.
