AB  - Women's socialization promotes supportive, socioemotional, and passive roles in American society. Supportive, caretaking roles in the society limit women's freedom of expression and place women in vulnerable positions, both economically and psychologically. There is evidence that female socialization predisposes women to greater incidences of depression, eating disorders, and certain personality and anxiety disorders. Violence against girls and women is associated with emotional disturbance. Because gender socialization is imbedded in every aspect of culture, individuals and systems operate to sustain cultural norms. Counselor bias can occur when counselors function to maintain cultural norms which inhibit women's free expression. In mixed-sex group situations (including group therapy) women may conform more to expected gender role behavior which may limit therapeutic growth. There has been relatively no empirical research on the benefits of all-female therapy groups with current knowledge being based on observations and case reports. Most observers conclude that all-female groups promote less stereotypical behavior in women clients thereby promoting free expression of a range of behaviors. Forty-eight women, who were currently being treated in either mixed-sex groups (25) or all-female groups (23) in Northern New Jersey out-patient settings were tested on three hypotheses: (1) will women treated in all-female groups report higher levels of assertiveness, self-esteem, and self-efficacy than women treated in mixed-sex groups, (2) will women treated in all-female groups report more helpful group process variables (universality, altruism, interpersonal learning, insight, cohesion, and catharsis) than women treated in mixed-sex groups, and (3) will women treated in all-female groups report more liberal views of female gender roles than women in mixed-sex groups. Instruments utilized were Rathus Assertiveness Schedule, Coopersmith Self-Esteem Inventory, Self-Efficacy (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
