AB  - Background Thousands of children attempt to emigrate to the U.S. each year from Latin America. Many have experienced significant trauma, and the process of reunification with families after prolonged separation is a known stressor. School-based group therapy has the potential to connect this vulnerable population with mental health resources. Through a collaboration with school-based caregivers, therapists, and pediatricians, a five-module curriculum for recently immigrated youth called FUERTE (Family Reunification and Resiliency Training) was created. After refining FUERTE's modules using stakeholder focus groups, the program was successfully piloted across schools city-wide and now ready for expansion. Objective To enroll 4 groups of immigrant youth participants in the FUERTE curriculum (at least 32 participants), each group with at least 5 hours of direct intervention. Once enrolled, FUERTE's goals were to increase wellness and resiliency amongst the students, as well as access to mental health services. Methods FUERTE workshops consisted of 5 weekly 1-hour sessions led by trained mental health providers focused on building resiliency. To date, 45 community health workers have been trained to facilitate the curriculum. The impact of the FUERTE program is measured through quantitative analysis to determine improvement in the following areas prior to the program and 3-months after completion of program, including self-reported mental and emotional well-being on validated scales (Pediatric Symptom Checklist-17 and the Resiliency Scale-14), self-reported enrollment in health insurance and access to health care services, as well as school attendance and GPA. Results FUERTE was piloted in Spring 2016 with two groups at 1 high school, and expanded in Fall 2016 to 3 high schools with a total of 34 youth (12 females and 22 males ages 14-19) participants. Pre-test and post-test scores have been collected from 9 participants; remaining pre- and post-test scores are in the process of being collected. The average participant Pediatric Symptom Checklist 17 (PSC-17) score, a validated psychosocial screening tool, prior to participation in FUERTE was 16.2, indicating the presence of significant psychosocial dysfunction (positive screen is score >15). T-test analysis revealed that the average participant PSC-17 score decreased from 16.2 to 11.1 (p=0.04) over the 5-week curriculum for the 9 participants. No significant difference observed before and after implementation of FUERTE using the Resiliency Scale-14. Further data on PSC-17 scores, Resiliency Scale scores, GPA, and attendance are pending. Conclusions Recently immigrated youth from Latin America represent a high-risk population for significant psychosocial dysfunction. Initial data support that participation in the FUERTE program is associated with improved psychosocial functioning evidenced by a significant decrease in PSC-17 scores. The FUERTE project will expand to be included in 5 high schools and 2 middle schools in Spring 2017. The finalized FUERTE curriculum will be disseminated on a national scale for open adaptation.
