AB  - In two centres for treatment of drug addicts, 98% of the patients admitted were examined for HIV antibodies (N = 247). 14% of the patients were HIV-positive and 1% of the patients had Aids. The psychological state of the HIV-positive patients was compared with that of a parallel group of HIV-negative patients. At the beginning of treatment there was hardly any difference between the two groups with respect to their psychological state. The rate of premature termination and the average duration of therapy were the same for both groups. The significant improvement in the psychological state of the HIV-positive patients in the course of treatment is possibly due to the additional therapy this group received. A brief description is given of the psychotherapy designed for the HIV-positive patients.
