AB  - Introduction: Twenty-three million Americans have a substance use disorder (SUD), yet only 10% receive treatment annually. Among hospitalized patients, 22% have an active drug or alcohol use disorder. Several interventions have demonstrated efficacy in improving outcomes for hospitalized patients with SUD, including offering a range of services to non-treatment-seeking patients, brief intervention for unhealthy drinkers, initiating pharmacotherapy, and bedside assessment with motivational interviewing and facilitated treatment referral. The challenge is implementing these interventions into real-world hospital practice. Method(s): This prospective, longitudinal study compared the 30-day post-discharge outcomes of patients who were or were not seen by an addiction consult team during their hospitalization. The study enrolled adult patients admitted to 14 general medical floors with an AUDIT-C of 8 or higher, a positive NIDA single question drug screen, or identified to have SUD by nursing assessment. The addiction consult team was available on 12 of 14 floors. The decision to consult the addiction team was made by the primary clinicians independent of this study. Intervention patients were those who were evaluated by the consult team and control patient were those who were not. Participants were assessed in-person at baseline and 30 days later by telephone or mail. Primary outcomes were the addiction severity index composite scores for alcohol and drugs and self-reported days abstinent 30 days posthospitalization. Secondary outcomes included the percentage of patients with self-reported emergency room visits and hospitalizations in the 30 days post index hospitalization. Analysis: Regression models were used to compare the 2 groups controlling for baseline differences. Result(s): Among the 265 who completed 30-day follow-up, 165 received the intervention and 100 did not. The mean age was 48 (SD 14) and 67% males. The groups differed significantly on several key demographics; those in the intervention group were more likely to be female (37% vs 25%, P = 0.037), unemployed (52% vs 25%, P< 0.001), and active smokers (79% vs 58%, P< 0.001). Baseline ASI composite scores were also higher in the intervention group (ASI-alcohol 0.46, ASI-drug 0.13, P< 0.001) than in the control group (ASI-alcohol 0.28, ASI-drug 0.10, P = 0.057). Primary and secondary outcomes improved in both groups; however, the magnitude of improvement was greater in the intervention group for all outcomes: mean ASI-alcohol decreased by 0.24 versus 0.08 (P < 0.001); mean ASI-drug decreased by 0.05 versus 0.02 (P < 0.001); days abstinent increased 12.7 versus 5.6 (P< 0.001); self-reported inpatient admissions decreased 73% versus 67% (P = 0.001), and ED visits decreased 67% versus 53% (P = 0.002). Self-reported overdose decreased by 93% versus 78% (P = 0.86). The differences in ASI-drug and days abstinent remained statistically significant after controlling for demographics and baseline values from the regression analyses. Conclusion(s): Among hospitalized general medical patients with SUD, inpatient addiction consultation decreased severity of drug use and increased number of days abstinent at 30 days posthospitalization.
