AB  - Lost productivity in business and industry heads most economists' lists of the costs of problems associated with abuse of alcohol and other drugs, not to mention other compulsive behaviors and difficulties with coping in general.2 Further, it is widely believed that, unacknowledged and untreated, these problems lie at the root of a great many ostensibly "medical" expenditures by employees and their dependents. [...]while the direct costs of treating chemical dependency and mental illness are a very legitimate concern, employers perceive that failing adequately to treat these problems may give rise to indirect costs that ought to be of even greater concern. At a Pew Health Policy conference last December, sponsored by the Boston University Center for Industry and Health Care, twenty-six senior managers from major national corporations met with academic researchers from several disciplines, clinicians, and other resource people to address the question of how companies should structure and manage the financial coverage they provide to help employees (as well as retirees and dependents) overcome chemical dependency and mental illness. The manager of employee benefits for one company that extends coverage to a limited list of outpatient mental health services provided by a psychiatrist said his staff occasionally receives and denies a claim for an uncovered service (say "a marital problem"), whereupon, by return mail, they "get a letter back saying, 'Oh Gosh, if you don't like that, would you take an anxiety reaction?"' Again they deny the claim, all the while strongly suspecting that other psychiatrists are playing similar diagnostic games, but with greater finesse. Partici' pants included Pew Corporate Fellows: James Adams, Sun Company; Mary Carmichael, Citibank; Robert Carpenter, LTV Corporation; Sidney Curtis, M.D., United Technologies Corporation; Robert Dedmon, M.D., Kimberly-Clark Corporation; Donald Flagg, Stouffer Corporation; David Gluck, M.D., J.C. Penney Company, Inc.; Richard Hanley, Owens-Illinois, Inc.; George Hanshaw, U.S. Steel Corporation; Dorothea Johnson, M.D., AT&T Communications; Benjamin Katcoff, Polaroid Corporation; Richard Kennedy, General Electric Company; Helen Lewis-Wistner, W.R. Grace & Company; Calvin Michaels, Burlington Industries; James Mitchell, M.D., Eastman Kodak Company; Albert Ritardi, Allied Corporation; John Scott, Aluminum Corporation of America; Robert Shaughnessy, AT&T Technologies; Lloyd Tepper, M.D., Air Products & Chemicals, Inc.; Richard Wright, Caterpillar Tractor Company.
