AB  - Discusses the essential leader responsibilities for group work with seriously mentally ill participants in an effort to detail practice interventions for social work group leaders. Key leadership tasks relevant to the issues of member heterogeneity, relationships between core and new members, confrontation, members risk taking, and leader emphasis on long-term vs short-term member concerns are addressed. Illustrations from a weekly group for 3–6 schizophrenics held at an urban shelter are provided. This group was an interaction-oriented group with the goals of understanding mental illness, improving coping with symptoms, building self-esteem, and mutual support. Leaders of such groups should be able to deal with the inability of the group to progress toward cohesive stages, be comfortable with ambivalence, and be able to accept clients at the current level of functioning. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
