AB  - Background: Dementia has become a growing public health concern in China. Evidence has emerged that multidomain interventions may help preserve cognitive function in elderly people. We presented the baseline assessments of the ongoing randomized controlled multimodal intervention study to delay dementia and disability in rural China (MIND-CHINA). Method(s): MIND-CHINA targets individuals who are aged 60-79 years and living in the rural communities of Yanlou Town, western Shandong Province. The multimodal intervention program consists of four modules that are sensitive to Chinese lifestyle and sociocultural tradition: lifestyle and nutritional guidelines; management of vascular risk factors (i.e., high blood pressure, high blood glucose, and high cholesterol); social, physical, and intellectual activities; and cognitive training. Result(s): The baseline examination that was integrated with the annual National Primary Health Care Services was completed in March-September 2018, during which 5752 persons were examined. At baseline, the following data were collected through face-to-face interviews: clinical examination, cognitive and physical testing, epidemiological data (e.g., smoking, alcohol consumption, sleep habits, and leisure activity), clinical data (e.g., medical history, neurological disorders, hearing impairment, and chronic diseases), physical functioning (assessed with the short physical performance battery test and activities of daily living), cognitive function (assessed with comprehensive cognitive test battery), and diagnosis of mild cognitive impairment and dementia. Following the baseline examination, 52 villages in Yanlou Town will be cluster-randomized into three groups for interventions: control group (regular primary health care service), intensive vascular intervention group, and multimodal intervention group. Recruitment of the eligible participants for the intervention programs are ongoing. Conclusion(s): It is feasible to integrate a comprehensive health examination program with local primary health care services in rural-dwelling elderly Chinese people. MIND-CHINA will be likely to address whether multimodal interventions sensitive to Chinese lifestyle and sociocultural tradition help maintain cognitive and physical functioning among community-dwelling elderly people in rural areas.Copyright © 2019
