AB  - Compared the clinical effectiveness of general practitioner care and 2 general practice-based psychological therapies for depressed patients. A prospective, randomised controlled trial was conducted with 464 patients presenting with depression or mixed anxiety and depression. Ss were exposed to either usual general practitioner care or up to 12 sessions of non-directive counselling or cognitive-behaviour therapy provided by therapists. The main outcome measures were Beck depression inventory scores, other psychiatric symptoms, social functioning, and satisfaction with treatment measured at baseline and at 4 and 12 months. Results show that all groups improved significantly over time. At 4 months, patients receiving non-directive counselling or cognitive-behaviour therapy improved more in terms of the Beck depression inventory than those receiving usual general practitioner care. However, there was no significant difference between the 2 therapies. There were no significant differences between the 3 treatment groups at 12 months. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
