AB  - Improvisation is a performative process not typically offered in inpatient and outpatient settings for people undergoing treatment for co-occurring substance and alcohol abuse disorders ('SUDS'). The effects of improvisation on communication between participants who attended two facilities—a residential inpatient treatment center and an intensive outpatient day program for co-occurring SUDS—were explored in this study. Eight workshops occurred at each facility, 16 total. The workshop structure included (a) breathing, centering, member check-in; (b) warm-ups; (c) theatre games/improvisations; (d) member check-in; (e) storytelling and/or improvisational skit creation; and (f) member check-in. Fifty-six consent forms were obtained from 44 males and 12 females ranging in age from 19–60. Data collection involved participant journals, interviews, a teacher journal, and demographic forms. Twenty-nine journals were collected, and 18 one-to-one interviews were conducted with participants who attended 1–8 workshops. One focus group interview spontaneously emerged during one workshop.Eight themes emerged from eight workshops at both facilities. Five themes that emerged after one workshop were: (a) staying present; (b) laughter; (c) empathy; (d) getting out of my shell; and (e) improved social interactions. For those who continued improvisational training at the residential inpatient facility, three more themes were added: (f) no mistakes; (g) freedom to be myself (or someone else) and not care what others think, and (h) group mind. The same eight themes emerged at outpatient day programming, but the fifth theme, improved social interactions, predominated. Social interactions increased, as well. Therefore, the fifth theme of increased and improved social interactions at the outpatient day program was explored through 4 different lenses: (a) increased and improved social interaction reduced social anxiety and lifted the spirit; (b) increased and improved social interactions through storytelling; (c) increased and improved social interactions through the introduction of new games; and (d) increased and improved social interactions at sober living.Participant observers noted that the teaching artist was not facilitating therapy. Conceptual change and five basic needs models served as general frameworks for data gathering. Unconditional positive regard for all the participants, the improvisational tools of moving the story forward, and making one's partner look good framed facilitation. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
