AB  - Objectives: How do multiethnic adolescents respond to intensive outpatient treatment (IOP) compared with their single ethnicity-identifying peers? By exploring variations in each group's symptoms over the course of our program, we aimed to better understand the experience of mixed-ethnicity teenagers in treatment so that we could provide more targeted support in the future. Given the current hypotheses about cultural and familial stress contributing to mixed children's increased vulnerability to mental illness, we expected to see differences in our patients' reporting of relationship stress over time. Method(s): All subjects aged 12-18 years who attended an IOP for adolescents between June of 2015 and May of 2019 (n = 304) were used for this analysis. Subjects were separated based on self-reported ethnicity, and patients of mixed ethnicity were compared against all others on multiple variables. Result(s): The subjects' average age was 15.3 +/- 0.3 years. Females were 62.1 percent (188 of 304) of participants. Mixed-ethnicity patients made up 46 (15.2%) of the subjects and were the second largest ethnicity group (white 58.1%, Asian 11.9%, Hispanic 10.9%, and black 3.3%). Compared with other groups, mixed-race subjects reported more of a reduction in stress (mixed -7.0 +/- 26.8 versus other ethnicities 1.2 +/- 23.6, F = 4.3, p = 0.038), but a decline in their relationship with parents (mixed -3.2 +/- 20.1 vs. other ethnicities 3.7 +/- 21.9, F = 3.9, p = 0.05). Other measured symptoms showed no significant difference between groups. There were no differences on the beginning values for all categories of the Strengths and Difficulties Questionnaire (SDQ) by ethnicity for participants. Conclusion(s): Multiethnic children make up one of the fastest growing subsets of the US population, particularly in the San Francisco/Silicon Valley geographical area, yet little is known about why they are at higher risk for mental illness and how to specifically support them in treatment. Our IOP patients demonstrated more improvement in their overall stress level than their peers, despite showing worsening relationships with their parents. This may suggest a particular responsiveness to this group-oriented format of treatment among multiethnic teenagers, as well as a need for additional individualized family work to supplement our program. TREAT, ETHN, DEMFCopyright © 2019
