AB  - Compared the efficacy and safety of bupropion sustained release (BSR) with the selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor paroxetine (PA) in the treatment of major depression in elderly outpatients. 100 Ss (aged 60–88 yrs) with major depressive disorder were randomly assigned to treatment with BSR, 100–300 mg/day (48 Ss), or PA, 10–40 mg/day (52 Ss). Efficacy was assessed by changes in scores on the Hamilton Rating Scales for Depression and Anxiety and the Clinical Global Impressions-Severity of Illness and -Improvement scales (W. Guy, 1976). Safety was assessed by monitoring adverse events, vital signs, and body weight. Measurements of efficacy were similar between the 2 treatment groups, with both groups showing improved scores on all depression rating scales. Headache, insomnia, dry mouth, agitation, dizziness, and nausea occurred in >10% of Ss in both groups; somnolence, diarrhea, constipation, and anorexia occurred in >10% of Ss in the paroxetine group. No group differences in vital signs or weight were found. Both BSR and PA were safe and effective for the treatment of depression in the elderly. Because of its favorable side effect profile, BSR may provide a safe and effective nonserotonergic treatment alternative that is well suited as an antidepressant for the elderly. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
