AB  - Background/Purpose: Rates of depression are disproportionately high among cancer patients and survivors. In particular, individuals from traditionally underserved populations (including Hispanic/Latino, low income, rural, and elderly cancer patients) report significant depressive symptoms and have less access to evidence-based interventions. Here, we describe our efforts and goals to expand a collaborative care intervention (Collaborative Oncology Project to Enhance Depression Care; COPE-D), with particular focus on meeting the needs of underserved patients. The components of COPE-D include depression care management for patients with scores of >8 on the PHQ-9. Care Management includes (1) education about depression, (2) shared decision making for patients to decide on preferred intervention, including brief interpersonal counseling and medication management, (3) tracking PHQ-9 scores weekly until 50% reduction in the score (with revision of the treatment plan for those not achieving reduction in depressive symptoms). Method(s): An initial needs assessment included engagement with cancer center and community stakeholders to adapt the COPE-D protocol and its delivery. Implementation-specific program goals include (1) Increasing the reach of psychosocial services for underserved cancer patients; (2) Reducing depressive symptoms and other psychosocial outcomes among cancer patients who engage in the intervention. Result(s): Results of the initial needs assessment focused on the importance of cultural and regional competence, along with the need to expand bilingual staff and services. Training in the delivery of brief interpersonal counseling was also identified as a significant need. In addition to efforts to address these needs, anticipated results include a multilevel assessment of COPE-D implementation facilitators and barriers, along with an assessment of depressive symptoms among participants. Conclusions and Implications: Thoughtful expansion of the COPE-D intervention has significant promise to increase access to evidencebased psychosocial care and alleviate depressive symptoms for underserved cancer patients. In addition to its current implementation, continuing efforts focus on maximizing sustainability of the intervention.
