AB  - Describe the concepts and techniques of interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) and its current status of adaptation, efficacy data, and training. Topics discussed include: practice guidelines (American Psychiatric Association practice guidelines, primary care guidelines); overview of IPT (phases of treatment); use of IPT in mood disorders (acute treatment of major depression, predictors of response, continuation or maintenance treatment, geriatric depressed patients, bereavement-related depression, depressed adolescents, depressed adolescent mothers, depressed HIV-positive patients, depressed primary care patients, conjoint IPT for depressed patients with marital disputes, antepartum and postpartum depression, dysthymic disorder, bipolar disorder); use of IPT in other disorders (substance abuse, bulimia, group format for bulimia, social phobia, social phobia in a group format, panic disorder, body dysmorphic disorder, chronic somatization in primary care patients, borderline personality disorder, other applications); interpersonal counseling [ICP] (distress, subsyndromally depressed hospitalized elderly patients, ICP by telephone); IPT patient guide; and translations. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
