AB  - This study reveals the demographic and clinical characteristics of older adults seen in therapy at the Psychological Services Center (PSC) of the California School of Professional Psychology at Alameda. Records of 113 clients, age 50 and above, constituted the data source. Twenty-one of the clients were seen in community settings devoted to providing services for elderly people, the others were seen in PSC clinics. Demographic, clinical, and discharge variables were analyzed in relation to gender, age, and service site. The majority of older clients were age 50-59, female, Caucasian, and presented with relationship or emotional difficulties. The most common diagnosis was mood disorder. Most clients had an out-patient history and remained in therapy beyond the three-session intake. The majority were treated individually and were not given a referral at discharge. Most terminations were initiated by the client. Therapists determined that most clients improved but could benefit from further therapy. The mean number of sessions attended was 12.8, the mean number of weeks in therapy was 19.2, and the mean number of no shows/late cancellations was 1.3. In the full sample, men were more likely than women to be married or coupled and to be living with a spouse or partner. Older clients, age 60-99, stayed in therapy longer and were less likely to be seen as needing further treatment than the younger group. In the clinic subsample, older clients, age 60-79, had higher incomes and were more likely to be self-referred. Oakland clients had lower incomes, were more likely to have one or more disability, were less likely to live with a spouse or partner, and were less likely to be in group therapy for domestic violence. Berkeley clients had more education. Alameda clients had more income. Although under-represented in the client population relative to their numbers in the general population, older adults were conscientious, reliable, and used therapy effectively. Most of the people aged 60 and over were treated in specialized programs at locations where clients live or socialize, suggesting that clinicians can best provide services to them through active outreach in these natural settings. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
