AB  - Using long-term DUI (driving under the influence of alcohol) arrest recidivism data from a controlled study of DUI intervention effectiveness, interactions among DUI interventions, age, race, education, & alcohol severity were estimated via logit analysis of data collected in a 9-year follow-up study of the Mississippi DUI Probation Project (final N = 3,425 offenders). The effects of short-term interventions (alcohol education school for low alcohol severity offenders & structured group interventions for high alcohol severity offenders) were specified by educational level. Short-term rehabilitation was modestly effective for those with less than 12 years of education, but less effective or detrimental for the more highly educated. Probation was more effective for those under age 30 or 55+ than for the middle-aged group, & most effective for well-educated older (55+) offenders. Probation was also especially effective for young well-educated minority offenders. 4 Tables, 27 References. Modified HA
