AB  - The opioid epidemic has reached crisis levels in the United States. The economic cost of this epidemic was estimated at $504 billion in 2015, or 2.8 percent of the total gross domestic product. The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved the use of buprenorphine and methadone to treat opioid use disorders. Both have been recognized as essential and cost-effective treatments associated with abstinence and reduced opioid use and opioid-related overdose deaths. However, using methadone for opioid use disorders is restricted to a limited number of specialty opioid treatment programs certified by the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration. Buprenorphine, on the other hand, can be prescribed by office-based physicians under a waiver created by the Drug Addiction Treatment Act (DATA) of 2000 and its amendments in 2006 and 2016.5Using nationally representative data, our study highlights significant increases in the numbers of office-based visits involving a buprenorphine prescription among primary care and specialist physicians in the period 2006-14. The findings suggest that the previously reported upward trends in the provision of buprenorphine treatment may have been driven by increased involvement of physicians outside of psychiatry.
