AB  - Introduction: Young adults are a vulnerable population to developing substance use disorders. They are burdened with developmental vulnerabilities, have limited engagement in clinical care, and suffer from high rates of psychiatric comorbidity. Although medication-assisted treatment, such as extended release naltrexone (XR-NTX) can drastically protract abstinence, rates of retention are poor. Prior research has estimated that among adults with opioid use disorder (OUD) who receive XR-NTX during an inpatient detoxification, few continue with XR-NTX long-term. Assertive outreach interventions are commonly used to treat populations with low rates of medication compliance, such as individuals with schizophrenia, and are a promising approach to increase retention among individuals with OUD. This randomized, comparative effectiveness pilot study seeks to test the feasibility and effect of an additional psychosocial intervention using assertive community outreach on treatment retention among youth seeking treatment for OUD. Method(s):We are currently recruiting youth ages 18 to 26 years (N=40) who are seeking treatment for OUD from a community-based inpatient residential unit and willing to receive XR-NTX. Participants are randomly assigned to either treatment as usual (TAU) or the Youth Opioid Recovery Support (YORS) intervention group and followed for 24-weeks postenrollment. The TAU group is offered XR-NTX and care coordination for involvement in intensive outpatient psychotherapy postdischarge. The YORS group receives additional support including family engagement and involvement in treatment, assertive outreach (eg, telephone, text messaging, social media), contingency management incentives for receipt of XR-NTX doses, and home delivery of doses. The primary outcome is number of presence of a relapse event defined as 10 days of use within a 28-day period. Additionally, we measure number of XR-NTX doses received during the intervention and engagement in intensive outpatient treatment services. Result(s): Data analyses were conducted on the first 37 participants (18 YORS and 19 TAU) out of an expected 40 who have completed 12 weeks out of the expected 24 weeks of the study. Thirty-three percentage of participants in the YORS group and 74% of those in TAU met criteria for a relapse event (P=0.04). Participants in the YORS group also received significantly more outpatient doses of XR-NTX (M=2.1) compared with the TAU group (M=0.4; P<0.001). Engagement in intensive outpatient treatment among participants in the YORS group (M=11.4 sessions) was comparable with those in the TAU group (M=11.7 sessions). Conclusion(s): Preliminary results from our study suggest that the YORS treatment intervention consisting of family engagement, assertive outreach, contingency management, and home delivery of XR-NTX yielded superior treatment outcomes compared with TAU. Specifically, our intervention group had fewer relapse events and received more outpatient doses of XR-NTX compared with participants in the TAU group, suggesting that an assertive outreach framework is applicable and beneficial to providing treatment for OUD. Future research should examine, which components of the intervention are most meaningful to improving outcomes.
