AB  - In 2005, the Washington State legislature passed a law, (SB) 5763, to provide funding ($30 million) for the Treatment Expansion Program (TEP). This program expanded access to substance abuse (SA) treatment for adults on Medicaid and General Assistance (GA) clients. Of the funding provided through SB 5763, 65% came from expected cost savings in the Medicaid budget. We conducted a costoffset study to determine the cost savings associated with the TEP. Using administrative claims data, we constructed 3 expenditure measures that served as the outcomes for our study: (1) total medical expenditures, including all expenses for inpatient and outpatient services and pharmacy; (2) expenditures on long-term care services; and (3) mental health expenditures for inpatient mental health care (not including care provided at state psychiatric hospitals). We used a multi-step procedure to identify study groups for our analysis and to construct the data set. We identified all clients needing SA treatment based on diagnostic data or arrest data over a 7-year period beginning July 2001 (n=227,759). Of this group, 104,105 (45.7%) received SA treatment (inpatient or outpatient) and became eligible for inclusion in the study. Clients needing SA treatment but not receiving it became eligible for inclusion in the study's comparison group. To be included in the study, clients had to have had at least one month eligibility for Medicaid or GA in the pre-period (before June 2006) and in the post period (after July 2006). We constructed per member per month (pmpm) expenditure measures, which accounted for varying lengths of eligibility, for the 24-month baseline period and 24-month follow-up period for each client. Our study groups were as follows: (i) Medicaid adult disabled clients-treatment group (n=15,603), comparison group (20,655); and (ii) GA clients-treatment group (n-4,582), comparison group (n=3,653). We conducted a series of regression analyses that controlled for demographic factors, health risk score, baseline expenditures, index month and months eligibility in baseline period, and weighted the observations by the number of months of eligibility in the follow-up period. SA treatment was found to be associated with lower pmpm expenditures for all measures (P <.001). For Medicaid clients and GA clients, respectively, the estimates for total medical expenditures were $386 pmpm and $160 pmpm (P <.001). Mental health inpatient pmpm expenditures were also lower for Medicaid clients receiving SA treatment (-$12pmpm, P <.001). The estimated aggregate cost savings associated with SA treatment ($16.8 million) fully offset the costs of expanding access to treatment through the TEP over the study period.
