AB  - An extensive literature review was conducted on the various models and therapeutic approaches for working with gay, lesbian, and bisexual youth. Research was also reviewed on various risk factors gay youth face such as suicide, depression, and substance abuse. Studies were found showing a need for more services for gay and lesbian youth. The literature review first relates general sexuality research to the various models of homosexual identity development. The review then addresses three risk factor prevention models: primary, secondary, and tertiary. Each prevention model is associated with a particular stage of homosexuality identity development and associated risk factors. A comprehensive psycho-educational and cognitive-behavioral program is then presented which reflects the key aspects of the theoretical and practical implications of the literature. A three-part intervention program is proposed incorporating primary, secondary, and tertiary risk factor prevention. Primary prevention targets all youth, to educate them about sexuality. The primary prevention program consists of a question/answer handbook to educate all youth about sexuality, especially youth who may be experiencing same-sex feelings. The secondary prevention program is developed for teachers and school personnel. The handbook is designed to inform school personnel about sexuality, especially ways that they can help gay youth deal with confusion, peers, self-doubt, and self-acceptance. The secondary prevention handbook provides sexuality information as well as referral information to provide to gay and lesbian youth. The tertiary program is designed for self-identified gay, lesbian, and bisexual youth. It is a 20 session structured group therapy program specifically addressing the needs and concerns of gay youth. The goal of the program is to educate all people about sexuality. This education should increase tolerance and acceptance of gay, lesbian, and bisexual youth and reduce risk factors such as depression and suicide. A broad goal of the program will be to increase openness, respect, empathy, and to reduce externalized and internalized homophobia. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
