Y1  - 2011///
AB  - Brief interventions (BI) are recommended for adults with alcohol misuse. The Veterans Affairs (VA) Healthcare system implemented a national performance measure (PM) for BI in FY08. A study of VA outpatients prior to the PM found that black and Hispanic patients with alcohol misuse were more likely than white to report receiving alcohol-related advice-a primary component of BI. Because PMs may reduce healthcare disparities, this study evaluated whether the association between race and receipt of alcohol-related advice varied based on PM implementation. Respondents to the VA Office of Quality and Performance's FY07 and FY08 patient satisfaction survey (SHEP) were eligible for this study if they self-reported black, Hispanic, or white race, screened positive for alcohol misuse and responded to a question about whether a VA clinician had offered alcohol-related advice in the past year. Timing of alcohol-related advice was designated as pre- or post-PM based on the visit date that triggered survey mailing (FY07 vs. FY08, respectively). Logistic regression models adjusted for demographics and alcohol severity estimated the prevalence of self-reported alcoholrelated advice among each group both before and after the PM. A race by time interaction was tested to evaluate whether the association between race and advice varied over time. Among FY07-08 SHEP respondents with alcohol misuse (n = 53,667), 8% were black, 6% were Hispanic, and 85% were white, and 42% overall reported receiving alcohol-related advice. Before the PM, the adjusted prevalence of reporting alcohol-related advice was 47% (95% CI 45-50%) for black, 43% (40-45%) for Hispanic, and 38% (38-39%) for white patients with alcohol misuse. After the PM, adjusted estimates were 49% (46-51%) for black, 48% (45-50%) for Hispanic, and 42% (41-42%) for white patients. Significant differences between all groups were observed in each time period (p-values all < 0.001). There was no significant interaction between race and time (p = 0.2829). Black and Hispanic patients with alcohol misuse were more likely than white to report receiving alcohol-related advice before and after performance measure implementation, and the association between race and alcohol-related advice did not vary based on time. Further research is necessary to determine whether there were decreases in racial/ethnic disparities in documented brief alcohol counseling after implementation of the PM.
