AB  - Research Objectives: To compare effectiveness of a 12-month home-based rhythmic movement-to-music (M2M) intervention and a M2M intervention with social networking (M2Mplus) in increasing physical activity and adherence compared to an attention control. To examine potential mediators (key Social Cognitive Theory constructs directly targeted by the intervention) and moderators of treatment efficacy.DesignThree-arm pragmatic trial with stratified randomization. Assessments occur at baseline, 3, 6, 9 and 12 months. Setting(s): Spain Rehabilitation Center and home. Participant(s): 648 adults with a mobility disability between the ages of 21 and 65 will be recruited at Spain Rehabilitation Center. Intervention(s): A multilevel home-based, movement-to-music (M2M) exercise program tailored to the functional level of PWD will be delivered via tablet. An additional intervention group will include social networking (M2Mplus).Main Outcome Measure(s)Changes in physical activity, adherence, and health outcomes. Self-report outcomes include self-efficacy, self-regulation, outcome expectations, social support, barriers to exercise, ability to participate in social roles and activities, anxiety, depression, fatigue, pain interference, physical function with mobility aid. Physical assessments include anthropometrics, balance, grip strength, repeated chair stands, 6 Minute Walk/Push Test, and timed up and go. Result(s): Not applicable at this time.Conclusion/DiscussionThis multi-arm RCT will test this eHealth platform for increasing physical activity in PWD. If our results are positive, the clinic-to-home recruitment/exercise strategy has substantial potential for scale-up through the CDC-funded National Center on Health, Physical Activity and Disability.
