AB  - Undertreated pain symptoms interfere with quality of life and successful addiction treatment for people in opioid addiction treatment. Pain self-management programs have been recommended to promote non-pharmacological pain approaches, however, they remain inaccessible for many people due to cost, feasibility, and reliance on a biomedical treatment model that emphasizes pharmaceuticals. For those in addiction treatment, specialized pain care is rarely incorporated. We, therefore, piloted an online pain self-management program among 60 consenting adults who had a chronic pain diagnosis and were receiving opioid replacement therapy in an outpatient addiction clinic. Of 111 referrals to be screened to join our randomized controlled trial, 51 (45.9%) self-referred and 60 (54.1%) were referred after invitation from clinic staff. The majority (n = 44; 73%) reported their first use of opioids was from a legitimate painful event, while fewer reported first opioid use was for recreational purposes (n = 5; 8%) orfor psychological stress (n = 4; 6%). Of 31 assigned to an immediate treatment intervention group, 20 (64.5%) engaged in at least some online pain self-man-agement content. Mean program satisfaction ratings were favorable (> 5 on a 1 to 7 scale, where 7 = high satisfaction). Five (16%) in the treat-mentarm completed 100% of the assigned program content. Perceived benefits reported by participants included learning positive thinking, scheduling tasks, and tracking mood and activities. Reported barriers included difficulty navigating the program and poor internet access. Findings suggest that participants are receptive to online pain self-management, yet strategies are needed to increase engagement and maximize benefits. Supported in part byagrant from the Washington State University Alcohol and Drug Addiction Research Program.
