AB  - This article relates the development of an aggressive group therapy program in an outpatient, managed-care setting that formerly provided few group therapy opportunities. Clinical management's strategies to institutionalize these changes are explained, and types of groups, group goals, and staff responses are discussed. At the time of the writing of this article, the authors were managers and administrators at MCC Companies Inc. in Richmond, Virginia, one of central Virginia's largest pre-paid mental health and substance-abuse service providers. The overall goals of the agency are to provide accurate assessments of new patients and to match the type, intensity, and duration of treatment with the level of need. As pre-paid organization, an incentive exists to treat patients as efficiently as possible and to maintain or return them to a productive status as rapidly as feasible. The staffing pattern at MCC utilizes master's- and doctoral-level counseling professionals and psychiatric nurses. The philosophy of MCC promotes matching treatment resources to treatment needs. The agency practice at MCC had been to utilize group therapy primarily in treating substance abusers. Treatment took the form of an intensive outpatient treatment program involving patients and families in rehabilitation for one year.
