PY  - 1972
DA  - 1972/11//
Y1  - 1972/11//
AB  - This article focuses on the importance of social casework as an essential social service. In the last few years, the profession of social work and the field of social welfare have been encountering a series of stiff challenges. A key issue deals with the nature of the social services provided by the public and private sectors of the economy. The identification of public welfare workers as caseworkers regardless of the type of work they are doing clouds the issue and serves to antagonize the client group. It is well understood that personal social service have a direct relationship to the physical, mental and social well-being of individuals, families and communities. The growing complexity of the casework method is compounded by the move to identify a single social work method that proposes a comprehensive or integrated approach to social work practice and hence to social work education. Roots of social casework lie in social problem situations. Casework is a service that can be given to the poor and to the wealthy and to members of all ethnic groups. it has utility in any cultural environment that offers means for verbal and nonverbal communication. Casework can help illustrate the harmful effect of marginal incomes and lack of resources for poor families. It can also help point to the crisis-producing aspects of the life cycle that create social and family breakdown.
