AB  - Objectives: The objectives of this session are to describe the integration of early childhood mental health professionals in group-based pregnancy and well-child visits within a family medicine clinic and to discuss challenges related to sustainability and workforce development. Method(s): The session will discuss the integration of early childhood mental health providers and their role in offering prevention/health promotion, screening, consultation, and intervention services for pregnant women and young children in group-based primary care medical visits. Case examples will be included for illustration and discussion. Strategies to increase identification of mental health and developmental concerns, types of consultations provided, and ways to ensure successful referral completion for underserved populations will be discussed. The presentation will also cover workforce training approaches and the roles of psychologists and child and adolescent psychiatrists in pediatric settings. The content of the sessions will be described. Result(s): Over 260 women and 180 young children were served since integrating behavioral health providers into group medical visits. Integrating behavioral health providers aids in improving screening rates and connection to mental health and developmental supports. Challenges remain in developing sustainability for the model and addressing early childhood mental health workforce capacity. Conclusion(s): Integrating early childhood mental health professionals into group-based medical visits for underserved populations is a feasible way to increase access to a full continuum of mental health services. Advocacy is needed to support reimbursement, and continued emphasis should be on training early childhood mental health professionals and medical providers to work in diverse settings. EC, PRE, PATCopyright © 2020
