AB  - ''Needs'' and ''quality of life'' are very popular concepts nowadays. The two terms are used not only in policy documents, but they appear with increasing frequencies in the aims of ambulatory programs for people with psychiatric disabilities. The phenomena they refer to can be studied on two levels at least, that of the individual and that of groups or populations. In this article I focus on the individual level, because I want to explore what such promising concepts as quality of life and needs can mean to professionals in their daily work, and what role these notions can play in the innovation of care. Can we facilitate provider's efforts by offering them a framework to evaluate and improve their assessments and interventions? In this article I offer some of my preliminary findings and conclusions.
