AB  - Despite local and federal programs aimed at improving the use of prenatal care, research has indicated that a significant number of women still do not obtain adequate prenatal care. Most studies identify demographic characteristics as primary variables. The present study introduces depression and anxiety as primary variables. Eighty-one women receiving prenatal care at a community based clinic were divided into two groups based on the quality of prenatal care that they received. Chi square analyses revealed that, although ethnicity is the strongest predictor of prenatal care, depression and anxiety are significantly related to quality of prenatal care. The results of the regression analysis, in which depression remained significant (p = .038) and anxiety was nearly significant (p = .057), suggest that depression clearly has an effect on quality of prenatal care that is independent of the confounds. The findings provide useful information regarding interventions aimed at ameliorating this population's high incidence of receiving inadequate prenatal care. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
