AB  - Assessed 381 newly diagnosed cancer patients shortly after the time of initial diagnosis to study the effectiveness of preventive intervention in lowering emotional distress and improving coping. All Ss were over 18 yrs of age and had a prognosis of at least 4 mo. 59 Ss predicted by a screening instrument to be at risk for high levels of emotional distress and poor coping during the 2nd–6th mo of their illness were randomly allocated to 1 of 2 short-term intervention programs. Specific techniques to lower distress and improve coping were given during the 4 wks following diagnosis. A control group of 58 Ss, selected from those patients predicted to be at risk for low levels of emotional distress and poor coping, received no intervention. All were followed at 2–6 mo intervals by interview and testing. Assessment instruments included the Profile of Mood States, an index of vulnerability, an inventory of current concerns, and a measure of general coping strategies and problem resolution. Results show a significant lowering of emotional distress in the intervention group as compared to the controls. There was also a significant increase in the level of problem resolution in the intervention groups, although the numbers of problems experienced by both groups were no different. (10 ref) (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
