AB  - The current study examined the relationship between childhood sexual abuse (CXA), depression, and adult substance abuse among women. The study also investigated the relationship between CXA and substance abuse treatment recidivism as well as scores attained on a commonly used substance abuse assessment tool known as the Simple Screening Instrument for Alcohol and Other Drug Use (SSI-AOD). One hundred twenty-six women, between the ages of 18 and 64, participated in the study. Participants were selected from substance abuse treatment facilities, shelters and housing facilities, and a local college in the Washington metropolitan area. The subject body was 92% Black, 5% White, 1% Pacific Islander, and 2% Other. Participants completed a questionnaire which included a Psychosocial Background Questionnaire, the (1994) SSI-AOD, the (1996) Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), and the (1998) Childhood Trauma questionnaire (CTQ. Participants were assessed as CXA through self-report. Sexual abuse was defined as forced sexual intercourse including being forced to touch a sexual part of another person's body and/or a person having touched a sexual part of the participant's body against her will. Hypothesis one was fully supported in that depression was identified as a mediating variable between CXA and adult substance abuse through the use of Baron and Kinny's (1986) mediation design. Depression contributed 7% of the overall variance to adult substance abuse while childhood sexual abuse alone contributed an additional 1% of variance to adult substance abuse. By way of a one-way ANOVA, a positive relationship resulted between scores attained on the SSI-AOD and the self-reports of CXA, therefore, fully supporting hypothesis three. Hypothesis two was not supported. No relationship was found between number of substance abuse treatment episodes (recidivism) and CXA substance abusing women. This investigation suggests that highly skilled clinicians are needed to effectively assess and treat the complexities of substance abuse, CXA, and depression among women. Additionally, the current study's implications involving the treatment of CXA female substance abusers include recognizing the importance of using homogenous psychotherapy groups, providing psycho-educational interventions, and tailoring treatment interventions and plans to the specific needs of each woman. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
