AB  - High-quality relationships and interactions are key elements of effective social support. Research findings suggest that the perceived quality of support that a person receives from his or her social network is more important than quantity of support in terms of predicting several important health outcomes, including improved morbidity and mortality rates, stress, and depression. Certain characteristics of online support groups create opportunities that facilitate relationship development, maintenance, and ease of interaction, which can influence an individual's perceptions of the quality of social support. From research on offline social support it is known that both interactional and individual variables contribute to quality of supportive relationships, including homophily, self-disclosure, willingness to communicate about health, and sex. This chapter explores whether these factors are also relevant within online support groups. The authors survey online support community members from thirty health-related online support groups dealing with stigmatized health conditions (including alcoholism, cancer, HIV, and mental illness). 126 adults participated. Although the average scores on quality of online supportive relationships of our study are lower than those reported in studies of offline relationships, the reported quality is still remarkably high given the volatility of the interactions and the fact that these relationships can usually be classified as 'weak ties.' These scores imply that many individuals experience their social interactions within online support groups as meaningful and valuable.The chapter also discusses the theoretical and practical implications of the study findings as well as key limitations to the study and future directions for research in this area. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
