AB  - Utilizes a multidimensional impact & process evaluation framework to explore the DARE (Drug Abuse Resistance Education) program in terms of long-term effects, political potency, & implementation issues. Comparison of questionnaire data from 288 high school seniors exposed to DARE as seventh graders & 335 nonexposed seniors reveals no significant differences in drug use behaviors or attitudes. Microlevel process findings from a focus group interview reinforced the quantitative results. At the macrolevel, DARE represents a form of symbolic politics, supported by direct & indirect stakeholders, & embedded in a complex & potent organizational support structure. To minimize conflicts with DARE stakeholders & enhance program understanding, ways that factors associated with the implementation process may have influenced the results are explored, including contextual factors, disjunctions between program ideals & implementation realities, & program design limitations. This evaluation underscores the need for a more multifaceted discourse on drug prevention programs so as to consider the relative merits of both psychosocial & structural approaches. 3 Tables, 4 Figures, 88 References. Adapted from the source document.
