AB  - Findings are presented from a survey of administrators of 47 county departments of mental health & alcohol & drug programs in CA regarding services for individuals with co-occurring mental & substance abuse disorders. Findings indicate that collaboration across county mental health & alcohol & drug services primarily occurs through information sharing, coordination of services, & joint projects. Fewer than 50% of the counties responding provide integrated programs, & the most frequently provided services are outpatient counseling & case management. Administrators cited historical differences between the two service systems & societal stigma as the greatest barriers to service delivery. Two opposing strategies for state action were suggested, either establishing specific funding set-asides or blending funding for services. Counties varied widely in their ability to estimate unmet service needs. Implications for policy development related to the dually diagnosed are discussed. 5 Tables, 2 Figures, 15 References. Abstracted from source document.
