AB  - "Social change is slow; it requires persistence over time, one person, one action at a time. We are still working to realize the promise of Title IX." - Donna A. Lopiano, Executive Director Women's Sports Foundation 2002. Strong scientific evidence, based on a wide range of studies, shows that physically active people have higher levels of health-related fitness, a lower risk profile for developing a number of disabling medical conditions and lower rates of various chronic diseases than do people who are inactive.1,2,3 A large body of literature indicates that increases in physical activity and in turn fitness, are associated with improvements in health status.3,4 Regular physical activity has particularly significant health benefits such as maintaining independent living among older adults, yet persons over the age of 50 are among the most sedentary group of the population.5,6 Older women's participation in physical activity is suboptimal; for example, only 18% of women aged 45-64 years and 11% of women aged 65-74 years engage in strength training two or more times a week.2 Older women's attitudes towards physical activity are complex, arising from the interplay between psychological, social, environmental and economic factors.7 Physical activity and sport are social institutions that have developed within the backdrop of gender discrimination.8, 9 Systematic discrimination against girls and women is evident historically in social processes of socialization to sports participation, portrayal of gender in sports participation media images, females' access to sport and resources available to girls and women for physical activity and sport.8, 9 For example, until 1972 women were not allowed to run in marathons in North America.9 Changes to gender discrimination in sport in the US were catalyzed by the endorsement of Title IX (1972), the Patsy Mink Equal Opportunity in Education Act that states in part: “No person in the United States shall, on the basis of sex, be excluded from participation in, be denied the benefits of, or be subjected to discrimination under any education program or activity receiving federal financial assistance...”10 Thus, the current cohort of older women lived during formative years in which there was systematic gender discrimination in physical activity and sport. This exclusion further underpins the complex interaction between the social environmental and economic factors that are barriers to older women's participation in physical activity. The health benefits of physical activity have been explored in the literature for several decades. Citing articles published in the late 1980s and early 1990s Fletcher et al.,11 note that “developing and maintaining aerobic endurance, joint flexibility and muscle strength is important in a comprehensive exercise program, especially as people age.” These authors point out that elderly men and women show comparable gains in health benefits when adhering to exercise training, both resistance and aerobic endurance training. Weuve, Kang, Manson, Bretyler, Ware & Grodstein,12 conducted a longitudinal study using the Nurse’s Health Study data and showed that long-term regular physical activity, including walking, improved cognitive functioning and reduced cognitive decline in women 70-81 years. Almost a quarter of a century ago, findings from a study by Fiatarone et al., pointed to the importance of high-resistance weight training regardless of age by showing significant gains in muscle strength, size and functional mobility among frail residents of nursing homes up to 96 years of age.13 Findings indicated that strength training is even more important as individuals' age to improve functional capability and maintain community independence.14 Research has consistently demonstrated the benefits of physical activity to cognitive functioning in older adults.15 In 2001, 60% of the 56 million deaths annually and 47% of the global burden of disease were attributable to the risk factors of physical activity and diet; key risk factors for non-communicable diseases.16,17 Physical activity and diet have both a combined and separate effect on health. The burden of death and disability related to non-communicable diseases is greatest and continuing to grow in developing countries, while in developed countries, the overall burden of non-communicable diseases remains high.17,18,19 Rates of non-communicable diseases are increasing at an alarming rate globally.20 Recognizing that unhealthy diet and physical inactivity are the main risk factors for non-communicable diseases, regional consultations with UN member coun intergovernmental bodies, representatives of civil society and the private sector were held to develop a strategy for risk reduction for these risk factors.19,17 The goal of the WHO Global Strategy on Diet, Physical Activity and Health was to “promote and protect health by guiding the development of an enabling environment for sustainable actions at individual, community, national and global levels that when taken together will lead to reduced disease and death rates related to healthy diet and physical activity”.17 The WHO Strategy is inclusive of people across the life–span. Health benefits of physical activity for older adults include reduced risk of coronary artery disease, stroke, diabetes, hypertension, colon cancer, breast cancer and depression.21 It is notable that physical activity endows participants with physical, emotional and mental health benefits.22 As data accumulates about global health, the focus remains on the salience of physical activity to health. In 2008, the 61st World Health Assembly endorsed a resolution to implement the 2004 WHO Strategy, providing evidence-based recommendations for physical activity to guide implementation across all age groups and in diverse economic societies including low and middle income countries.21 Physical activity programs targeting older persons have been shown to improve health and physical activity levels in this population.5,6 In a critical review of 29 empirical studies, King, Resjeski and Buchner,5 concluded that there are a number of gaps in the current physical activity literature. In their discussion, the authors recommend undertaking systematic reviews of the literature to determine how best to design physical activity interventions targeting diverse subgroups of older adults. A search of both the Cochrane Library of Systematic Reviews and the Joanna Briggs Institute Library has been conducted. No systematic reviews of randomized controlled studies to promote physical activity for healthy older women living independently in the community are evident in the literature. However, a systematic review of the effects of physical activity in randomized controlled trials in healthy and “at risk” older adults has been registered with PROSPERO and was completed in December 2011.23 This review did not examine programs that specifically focus on healthy older women and address the unique challenges that contribute to their physical inactivity. The proposed systematic review fulfils all requirements for the PROSPERO data base. Definitions Physical activity is defined as: “Movement that increases heart rate and breathing and any bodily movement produced by skeletal muscles that requires energy expenditure”.24 In this systematic review, physical activity is defined as activities that focus on promoting bodily movement. This will include multior single-component programs: strength, balance, flexibility, aerobic fitness, yoga, tai chi, walking (pole walking), running, gardening, skiing, bicycling, swimming, dancing, and Wii fit (a popular computer game).25 Limits will not be imposed on duration or intensity of the physical activity intervention. A physical activity intervention is defined as an organized initiative or structured program designed to promote increased physical activity.
