A1  - Heider J
A1  - Schroder A
KW  - eppi-reviewer
PY  - 2008
DA  - 2008///
Y1  - 2008///
AB  - Somatoform symptoms are a prevalent and disabling condition in primary care practice, causing high medical care utilization. Objective: To compare the short-term efficacy of cognitive behavioral outpatient group therapy with a relaxation group and a waiting control group, in regard to physical symptoms, anxiety, depression, and functional health. Methods: 77 subjects were treated and assessed in a randomized control group design. The manualized 8-session cognitive-behavioral group intervention is based on Rief and Hiller's (1998) treatment approach. Results: Both cognitive-behavioral as well as relaxation group treatments lead to lower levels of somatoform symptoms (SOMS-7). Furthermore, cognitive-behavioral treatment reduced anxiety (HADS-G) and enhanced mental health (SF-12). Conclusions: This brief cognitive-behavioral group therapy has beneficial effects on out patients with somatoform symptoms that go beyond the effects of a relaxation group.
