AB  - Introduction The UN estimates that 3.55-5.7% of the world's population between 15 and 64 years of age use illegal drugs. High rates of co-morbidity between EDs and disorders caused by drug and alcohol use (SUDs) are reported. The prevalence of SUD in persons with ED is around 50%, as compared with 9% in the general population; on the other hand 35% of those with SUD also suffer from ED, as compared with 3% in the general population (1). The literature also highlights the concomitance of ED sub-types and SUD types. However, clinical observation also reveals a strong behavioural interaction between ingesting food and ingesting drugs. Such interaction includes food deprivation in persons with active drug addiction, weight increase during rehabilitative treatment for drug dependence and/or detoxification, recurrent bulimic symptoms previous to opiate addiction, and binge eating during replacement therapy for opiates, which thus highlights the extent to which ED/SUD disorders are interconnected and how the "gratification and reward" induced by food is a possible substitution factor for opiate dependency or may be prodromal to relapse (2). Case study The case of a 24 year-old young adult is representative. A tobacco smoker with normal weight, he had been under observation for heroin addiction since age 20. After withdrawal treatment at a specialised residential facility, and subsequent to replacement therapy for the purposes of abstinence stabilisation after opiate addiction, the patient entered a rehabilitation program at a live-in facility (community). This program employs a cognitive behavioural model for dealing with emotional dysregulation, impulsiveness and substance craving. Eating disorders began to surface as early as the first month of the program in the community. They included binge eating, night eating and weight gain. Community workers submitted a request for consultation to the ED centre. When the patient's history was taken, traumatic episodes and obesity at age 15 were uncovered. Discussion In the assessment of patient ED-SUD, attention must be paid to the adaptive function of drug use. The questions to be raised are: "What is the adaptive function of drug use in the patient's eating disorder?" ED symtoms are one potentially important factor that could be associated with treatment rejection among individuals in treatment for SUDs? Is the residential treatment the best for the co-occurrence of SUDs and EDs? The same holds true for assessment of SUD, in which the function of ED (generally acting as an emotional modulator in this situation) must be explored. In addition to clinical and nutritional interviews at the ED centre, the patient was given a battery of tests that included: BES, BUT, Millon and YFAS. The purpose of the evaluation was to answer the questions outlined above. Conclusions ED and SUD patients are often reluctant to verbalise their symptoms, as they feel shame and guilt. Medical/toxicological stabilisation of the patient must be viewed as the first step of psychological treatment as well. In such treatment, the building of a collaborative therapeutic relationship based on patient trust must be emphasised, along with a relationship of mutual trust among therapists engaged in multidisciplinary treatment. This prevents the power struggles that often occur between local services, between local services and/or hospital departments and accredited therapeutic communities, thus creating the therapeutic continuity that is vital to the treatment program and which is one of the critical elements in multidisciplinary treatments (3,4). Scientific evidence on the management of treatment of ED-SUD co-morbidity, and the strategies for such treatment, is rather scanty (3). In the specific case presented above, evaluation and treatment of ED during the rehabilitative program for drug addiction was decided upon. It was carried out by ED specialists, but in close cooperation with the therapists at the community. Treatment of this illness can consist of containing or controlling addictive behaviour and implementing rehabilitative strategies that help the addicted individual have new experiences which constitute alternatives to addictive behaviour patterns, and are just as gratifying.
