AB  - A cost-benefit perspective was used to investigate whether society spends more money punishing than it gains from punishment. Analysis of available data suggests that greater social benefits are derived from prison incarceration than are generally assumed, which indicates overwhelming support for more prison capacity. However, the case for the present use of probation and fines is less clear because less data are available on the application of these sanctions. However, it appears that social costs would be reduced if more probationers were given either prison terms or fines. Incapacitating borderline offenders now crowded out by space constraints would likely cost communities less in crime expenditure than they now pay in social damages and prevention. Punitive fines for first offenders may have a deterrent effect and would reduce expenditures on prison supervision while producing revenues and perhaps compensating victims.
