AB  - Aim: To investigate the status of coping style, personality situation, mental health and social support in heroin addicts. Method(s): Totally 133 heroin addicts (study group) in a forced drug rehabilitation center of Guangdong provinceand normal controls were tested with coping style questionnaire, social support questionnaire, Eysenck personality questionnaire (EPQ) and symptom checklist-90(SCL-90) from January 2003 to August 2004. Result(s): The scores of solving problem and seeking for help subscales in coping style were lower in the study group (0.635 +/- .200, 0.423 +/- 0.170) than in the control group, while those of self-blame, illusion, withdrawal and rationalization were higher in the study group (0.503 +/- 0.210, 0.481 +/- 0.160, 0.555 +/- 0.200, 0.470 +/- 0.180) than in the control group. In EPQ, the scores of extraversion and introversion (E) and neuroticism (N) in the study group (11.55 +/- 4.30, 16.47 +/- 4.56) were higher than those in the control group (9.85 +/- 4.36, 10.81 +/- 4.45), while the scores of psychoticism (P) and lie (L) were close between the study group (7.85 +/- 3.04, 11.54 +/- 3.89) and control group (7.86 +/- 3.05, 11.75 +/- 3.55). The subjective and objective supportive scores of social support questionnaire were lower in the study group (6.73 +/- 2.73, 19.77 +/- 4.80) than in the control group (9.03 +/- 2.79, 21.97 +/- 5.81). All the SCL-90 scores in the study group were all one average score higher than those in the control group. Conclusion(s): Heroin addicts tend to adopt the immature coping styles to face their problems, such as withdrawal, self-blame and illusion; all of them have serious mental health problems, emotional instability and bad social support.
