AB  - Background: The COVID-19 pandemic presented an extraordinary challenge to caring for patients with ESRD. Many home dialysis patients were forced to confine themselves to their homes where they experienced decreased social interaction. Loneliness, depression, and anxiety are all known consequences of social isolation, which all have detrimental effects on patients. Hypothesis: Hosting a virtual patient support group will help alleviate social isolation for home dialysis patients and may have other subjective benefits. Method(s): Since July of 2020, the social worker at Wellbound Emeryville has hosted an open monthly patient support group using Zoom to address this increase in social isolation. This support group secondarily aims to provide patients with an opportunity to network with fellow patients, share their experiences, and to connect to potential peer mentors. Result(s): As the result of the monthly Zoom support group, 12 patients and family members attended the sections. Although the attendance rates are not very high relative to the clinic population, those who attended reported that that the virtual support group meetings they attended have been very helpful and wish to continue the program. The patients expressed that they found it helpful to troubleshoot dialysis-related lifestyle changes with one another and that these groups offered them a sense or normalization and community. Conclusion(s): In the COVID era, social isolation is a serious problem for patients on home dialysis. Using virtual technology to support patient is one of the methods can be utilized and has been effective, however further study is needed to improve patient and family engagement. Future research should also address the potential benefits of separating out a virtual, caregiver-specific support group.
