AB  - Reviews data that indicate that alcoholism among the elderly is a significant and ignored problem. Classification of elderly alcoholics according to distinctions that allow differing prognoses and treatment approaches is discussed, especially the one made between early- and late-onset drinkers. Sociopsychological stresses during aging (e.g., depression, retirement, loneliness, marital stress, and physical illness) are believed to be important reasons for drinking in both early- and late-onset alcoholics and intervention efforts directed at these stresses, rather than the drinking behavior itself, may be most beneficial. Group socialization, social casework, family casework, and antidepressant medication and medical care when necessary; are among treatments recommended. Treatment intervention delivered through facilities serving the aged is recommended over alcoholism treatment programs. The concepts of the therapeutic community (e.g., open communication with staff, patient participation in the therapeutic and administrative aspects of treatment, and contacts with the community at large) can be extremely helpful when applied to the management of elderly institutionalized populations and are described in relation to actual communities studied. (25 ref) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
